window.BIBLIO_ARTICLES = [{"id": "pmid-42963497", "pmid": "42963497", "pmcid": "", "doi": "10.1007/s00256-026-05414-w", "title": "Temporal MRI appearance of postoperative healing after proximal hamstring musculotendinous complex repair", "title_fr": "Aspect IRM temporel de la cicatrisation post-opératoire après réparation proximale du complexe musculo-tendineux des ischio-jambiers", "authors_list": ["Mechó S", "Pérez P", "Congo A", "Isern-Kebschull J", "Alomar X", "Pruna R", "Valle X", "Yanguas X", "Moix I", "Puigdellívol J", "Lempainen L", "Aguilar M", "Pedret C", "Kassarjian A", "Rodas G"], "authors_short": "Mechó S, Pérez P et al.", "journal": "Skeletal radiology", "journal_short": "Skeletal Radiol", "impact_factor": 1.9, "year": 2026, "month_num": 10, "month_fr": "Oct.", "pub_date_fr": "Oct. 2026", "volume": "", "issue": "", "pages": "", "category_id": "imaging_bamic_mri", "category_label": "Imagerie IRM, Signe du Cobra, Tendon Central & BAMIC", "category_short": "IRM, BAMIC & Signe du Cobra", "category_color": "#6366f1", "modality": "IRM 3 Tesla", "intervention_type": "Suivi IRM Post-Opératoire", "cochin_link": "Étude de référence (Skeletal Radiology 2026, équipe Barça / Lempainen / Kassarjian) indispensable pour interpréter les IRM de contrôle post-opératoires chez le footballeur sans conclure à tort à un retard de cicatrisation.", "summary_fr_short": "Suivi longitudinal par 43 IRM 3T durant les 4 premiers mois après réparation chirurgicale chez 20 footballeurs professionnels. Des fentes intra-tendineuses résiduelles persistent dans ~40 % des cas malgré une cicatrice mature et une évolution clinique normale.", "summary_fr_detailed": "Lors du suivi IRM post-opératoire précoce (< 4 mois) des réparations proximales chez le footballeur professionnel, des fentes intra-tendineuses longitudinales (73,3 %) et transversales (86,7 %), une angulation tendineuse (100 %) et un œdème de dénervation musculaire (66,7 %) sont quasi constants initialement. Même lorsque la cicatrice devient mature en hyposignal, des fentes intra-tendineuses persistent dans ~40 % des tendons asymptomatiques : seules les cavités extensives en franc hypersignal DP-FatSat avec cicatrice immature signent une exacerbation clinique.", "protocol_fr": "IRM 3T séquentielle dans les 4 premiers mois post-opératoires • Analyse de la maturation du cal fibreux en T1/DP-FS sans sur-interpréter les fentes linéaires résiduelles de remodelage.", "key_points_fr": ["La présence de fentes intra-tendineuses (73–87 %) et d'un signal de dénervation (67 %) est habituelle dans les 4 premiers mois.", "Une fente résiduelle au sein d'un tendon remodelé en hyposignal mature ne contre-indique pas la progression vers le retour au jeu.", "Seule l'association fente large + cavité liquide + cicatrice immature corrèle avec une rechute clinique."], "abstract_en": "Objective To describe the temporal MRI features of healing during the first 4 months following surgical repair of complete tears of the proximal hamstring musculotendinous complex. Materials and methods Consecutive case series. Professional football players with MRI-confirmed complete tears surgically repaired between May 2017 and December 2025 were retrospectively identified. Postoperative MRI examinations were obtained during routine clinical follow-up using 3-T systems. Two musculoskeletal radiologists independently evaluated longitudinal and transverse intratendinous clefts, scar maturation, edema-like signal, postoperative subcutaneous soft-tissue signal change, tendinous angulation, blunt muscle outline, and denervation-like signal. Results Twenty-four complete tears in 20 players underwent surgical repair. Fifteen tendons with an uncomplicated postoperative course comprised the primary descriptive cohort, yielding 43 MRI examinations. Longitudinal intratendinous clefts were observed in 11/15 (73.3%) of tendons, transverse intratendinous clefts in 13/15 (86.7%), postoperative subcutaneous soft-tissue signal change and tendinous angulation in 15/15 (100%), blunt muscle outline in 13/15 (86.7%), and denervation-like signal in 10/15 (66.7%). In the within-tendon analysis, longitudinal and transverse intratendinous clefts resolved by the last available MRI in 6/10 (60%) and 7/12 (58.3%) tendons, respectively. All 10 tendons with an initially immature scar progressed to a mature scar. Among six tendons with postoperative exacerbation, four showed extensive, prominent intratendinous clefts with marked PD-FS hyperintensity on the preceding MRI, associated with immature scar, intratendinous cavity, or persistent interstitial edema-like signal. Conclusion Serial MRI demonstrated different temporal patterns of postoperative healing. Residual intratendinous clefts may coexist with a mature scar during tendon remodeling and do not necessarily indicate delayed healing.", "tags": ["IRM post-opératoire", "Cicatrisation tendineuse", "Football professionnel", "Skeletal Radiol 2026"], "pdf_file": null, "pdf_source": "external_link", "pdf_size_kb": null, "pubmed_url": "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/42963497/", "doi_url": "https://doi.org/10.1007/s00256-026-05414-w", "pmc_url": "", "article_url": "https://doi.org/10.1007/s00256-026-05414-w"}, {"id": "pmid-42702351", "pmid": "42702351", "pmcid": "", "doi": "10.1002/arj.70513", "title": "Structural Allograft Reconstruction for Chronic Proximal Hamstring Avulsion Yields Significant Functional Improvement, Acceptable Satisfaction, but Limited Return to Preinjury Sport Levels", "title_fr": "Reconstruction par allogreffe structurelle des avulsions proximales chroniques des ischio-jambiers : résultats fonctionnels à moyen terme", "authors_list": ["Lefèvre N", "Moussa MK", "Valentin E", "Bohu Y", "Gerometta A", "Khiami F", "Meyer A", "Grimaud O", "Hardy A"], "authors_short": "Lefèvre N, Moussa MK et al.", "journal": "Arthroscopy : the journal of arthroscopic & related surgery : official publication of the Arthroscopy Association of North America and the International Arthroscopy Association", "journal_short": "Arthroscopy", "impact_factor": 4.4, "year": 2026, "month_num": 9, "month_fr": "Sept.", "pub_date_fr": "Sept. 2026", "volume": "42", "issue": "11", "pages": "2508-2516", "category_id": "chronic_revision_allograft", "category_label": "Ruptures Chroniques, Révisions & Greffes (Allogreffes)", "category_short": "Chroniques, Allogreffes & Révisions", "category_color": "#8b5cf6", "modality": "Chirurgie Reconstructrice", "intervention_type": "Reconstruction par Allogreffe", "cochin_link": "Valide la reconstruction par allogreffe dans les ruptures chroniques irréparables directement : le score PHAS médian bondit de 56,3 à 84,5 à 3,4 ans de recul.", "summary_fr_short": "Sur 42 patients opérés par allogreffe structurelle pour avulsion proximale chronique rétractée (recul moyen 3,4 ans), le score PHAS médian progresse de 56,3 en pré-opératoire à 84,5 au dernier recul, avec une excellente récupération fonctionnelle quotidienne.", "summary_fr_detailed": "Lorsque la rétraction tendineuse chronique et la perte de souplesse musculaire empêchent une réinsertion directe sur l'ischion sans flexion excessive du genou, l'interposition d'une allogreffe structurelle constitue la solution de sauvetage. Sur cette cohorte suivie 3,4 ± 1,6 ans, l'analyse des seuils cliniques (MCID, SCB et PASS fixé à 82,3 sur le score PHAS) confirme un gain fonctionnel majeur et une bonne satisfaction des patients, bien que le retour au sport de compétition au niveau pré-lésionnel reste plus limité qu'après réparation aiguë.", "protocol_fr": "Abord longitudinal sous-fessier élargi • Neurolyse prudente du nerf sciatique • Fixation proximale solide de l'allogreffe sur la tubérosité ischiatique par ancres et suture distale en Pulvertaft/enveloppement sur le moignon rétracté.", "key_points_fr": ["Progression spectaculaire du score PHAS médian de 56,3 (IQR 49,0–64,0) à 84,5 (IQR 73,8–94,8).", "Plus de 56 % des patients franchissent le seuil PASS (Patient Acceptable Symptom State, PHAS > 82,3).", "Option fiable de sauvetage lorsque la réinsertion directe en extension/faible flexion est impossible.", "Information loyale du patient : excellente fonction quotidienne mais retour au sport intensif plus mesuré."], "abstract_en": "Purpose To evaluate midterm outcomes of structural allograft reconstruction in patients with chronic proximal hamstring avulsion injuries. Methods This was a retrospective cohort study (minimum 2-year follow-up) including patients who underwent allograft surgical reconstruction for proximal hamstring avulsion injuries between 2016 and 2023. The primary outcome was the Parisian Hamstring Avulsion Score (PHAS). Secondary outcomes included the Tegner Activity Scale, University of California, Los Angeles Activity Scale, return to sport rates, postoperative patient satisfaction, and complication rates. A cohort-specific minimal clinically important difference analysis was performed using the distribution-based method (½ standard deviation of the change in PHAS score). In addition, substantial clinical benefit and patient acceptable symptom state thresholds were determined using anchor-based methods. Results A total of 42 patients were included, with 39 having complete functional follow-up (mean follow-up: 3.4 ± 1.6 years). The PHAS score improved significantly from a median of 56.3 (interquartile range [IQR]: 49.0-64.0) preoperatively to 84.5 (IQR: 73.8-94.8) at the final follow-up (P 82.3) by 56.4%. Conclusions Allograft reconstruction is a viable option for chronic proximal hamstring avulsion injuries when direct repair is not feasible, yielding significant functional gains and acceptable satisfaction. Level of evidence Level IV, retrospective case series.", "tags": ["Allogreffe", "Rupture chronique", "Cohorte PHAS", "PASS / MCID", "Arthroscopy 2026"], "pdf_file": null, "pdf_source": "external_link", "pdf_size_kb": null, "pubmed_url": "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/42702351/", "doi_url": "https://doi.org/10.1002/arj.70513", "pmc_url": "", "article_url": "https://doi.org/10.1002/arj.70513"}, {"id": "pmid-42785966", "pmid": "42785966", "pmcid": "", "doi": "10.1136/bjsports-2026-111940", "title": "Sciatic nerve involvement is associated with longer return to sport in collegiate American football players with acute hamstring injuries", "title_fr": "L'atteinte œdémateuse du nerf sciatique en IRM allonge significativement le délai de retour au sport après lésion aiguë des ischio-jambiers", "authors_list": ["Lee KS", "Miller A", "Mosiman S", "Joachim MR", "Heiderscheit B"], "authors_short": "Lee KS, Miller A et al.", "journal": "British journal of sports medicine", "journal_short": "Br J Sports Med", "impact_factor": 11.8, "year": 2026, "month_num": 9, "month_fr": "Sept.", "pub_date_fr": "Sept. 2026", "volume": "", "issue": "", "pages": "bjsports-2026-111940", "category_id": "pht_endoscopy_sciatic", "category_label": "Tendinopathie Proximale (PHT), Endoscopie & Nerf Sciatique", "category_short": "Tendinopathie, Endoscopie & Sciatique", "category_color": "#ec4899", "modality": "IRM & Nerf Sciatique", "intervention_type": "Pronostic IRM Péri-Sciatique", "cochin_link": "Étude prospective (BJSM 2026) montrant que l'engainement œdémateux circonférentiel du nerf sciatique en IRM initiale est présent dans 41,7 % des lésions aiguës chez le joueur de football américain et retarde significativement le retour au jeu.", "summary_fr_short": "Sur 96 lésions aiguës des ischio-jambiers explorées par IRM en NCAA Division I, un contact œdémateux avec le nerf sciatique est observé dans 78,1 % des cas (36,5 % partiel, 41,7 % circonférentiel complet) et corrèle directement avec un délai de retour au sport prolongé.", "summary_fr_detailed": "En raison de la proximité anatomique intime entre le nerf sciatique et le complexe proximal/moyen des ischio-jambiers (notamment le long biceps fémoral et le semi-membraneux), l'hématome et l'œdème post-traumatiques diffusent fréquemment dans la gaine péri-nerveuse. Cette étude prospective démontre que l'œdème circonférentiel complet autour du nerf sciatique augmente avec le grade BAMIC et constitue un facteur pronostique indépendant d'allongement de l'indisponibilité sportive.", "protocol_fr": "Évaluation systématique sur les coupes axiales T2/DP-FatSat du rapport entre l'œdème musculaire et le nerf sciatique : absence de contact, contact partiel (< 180°) ou engainement circonférentiel complet (360°).", "key_points_fr": ["L'œdème entre en contact avec le nerf sciatique dans 78,1 % des lésions aiguës (dont 41,7 % d'engainement complet).", "Corrélation significative entre le grade BAMIC, l'engainement sciatique complet et le nombre de jours d'arrêt sportif.", "Justifie l'intégration d'exercices de mobilisation neurodynamique douce du nerf sciatique durant la rééducation."], "abstract_en": "Objective To assess if the British Athletics Muscle Injury Classification (BAMIC) grade and sciatic nerve involvement on MRI at time of hamstring strain injury (HSI) were associated with the time to return-to-sport (RTS) in National Collegiate Athletic Association Division I American football players. Materials and methods Data were collected via a larger prospective cohort investigation of collegiate football players who sustained an HSI. MRIs at time of injury were assessed by a musculoskeletal radiologist using BAMIC. Sciatic nerve involvement was characterised by extent of adjacent oedema on MRI as no contact, partial (contacted by oedema) or complete (circumferentially surrounded by oedema). Spearman rank correlations, mixed-effects modelling and χ 2 analyses were used to determine associations between imaging characteristics, HSI severity and days missed before RTS. Results 96 unique HSI were included (100% male, mean age 20±1.58 years). Increasing BAMIC grade was associated with longer time to RTS (r=0.29, p=0.004). Sciatic nerve involvement was observed in 75/96 (78.1%) cases (35/96 (36.5%) partial, 40/96 (41.7%) complete), and no sciatic nerve involvement in 21/96 (21.9%) cases. There was a significant positive association between sciatic nerve involvement as BAMIC grade increased (p Conclusions Increasing radiologic severity, indicated by BAMIC grade and complete sciatic nerve involvement were associated with longer RTS following HSI. Sciatic nerve involvement on MRI should be considered in the assessment of acute HSI.", "tags": ["Nerf sciatique", "IRM", "BAMIC", "Retour au sport", "BJSM 2026"], "pdf_file": null, "pdf_source": "external_link", "pdf_size_kb": null, "pubmed_url": "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/42785966/", "doi_url": "https://doi.org/10.1136/bjsports-2026-111940", "pmc_url": "", "article_url": "https://doi.org/10.1136/bjsports-2026-111940"}, {"id": "pmid-42530617", "pmid": "42530617", "pmcid": "", "doi": "10.1007/s00256-026-05319-8", "title": "Imaging characterization of sports-related hamstring injuries", "title_fr": "Caractérisation en imagerie des lésions sportives des ischio-jambiers : sémiologie IRM et classifications", "authors_list": ["Weber MA", "Randow M", "Klaan B"], "authors_short": "Weber MA, Randow M, Klaan B", "journal": "Skeletal radiology", "journal_short": "Skeletal Radiol", "impact_factor": 1.9, "year": 2026, "month_num": 7, "month_fr": "Juil.", "pub_date_fr": "Juil. 2026", "volume": "", "issue": "", "pages": "", "category_id": "imaging_bamic_mri", "category_label": "Imagerie IRM, Signe du Cobra, Tendon Central & BAMIC", "category_short": "IRM, BAMIC & Signe du Cobra", "category_color": "#6366f1", "modality": "IRM Diagnostique", "intervention_type": "Revue Sémiologique IRM", "cochin_link": "Synthèse radiologique complète 2026 (*Skeletal Radiology*) standardisant le compte-rendu IRM entre radiologues ostéo-articulaires, médecins du sport et chirurgiens orthopédistes.", "summary_fr_short": "Mise au point exhaustive sur la sémiologie IRM des lésions des ischio-jambiers chez l'athlète : topographie exacte (BFlh, ST, SM), atteinte de la jonction myo-tendineuse ou du tendon central, avulsions proximales et critères pronostiques de rechute.", "summary_fr_detailed": "Cet article de synthèse détaille les patterns lésionnels IRM des trois chefs ischio-jambiers selon le mécanisme traumatique (sprint haute vitesse touchant le long biceps fémoral vs étirement forcé en flexion de hanche/extension de genou touchant le semi-membraneux proximal). Il compare les classifications internationales (Munich, BAMIC, Barcelone) et insiste sur la description standardisée de l'atteinte tendineuse intra-musculaire, de la perte de tension et de la rétraction pour guider l'indication chirurgicale ou le délai de RTP.", "protocol_fr": "Protocole IRM Cuisse/Bassin : coupes axiales, coronales et sagittales T1 et DP/T2 Fat-Sat grand champ incluant la tubérosité ischiatique et la bifurcation distale.", "key_points_fr": ["Deux mécanismes distincts : sprint (chef long du biceps fémoral) vs étirement extrême (semi-membraneux proximal).", "Nécessité d'un langage standardisé entre radiologue et chirurgien (intégrité du tendon central, perte de tension, gap).", "Identification précoce des lésions à haut risque chirurgical (avulsions tubérositaires et ruptures du tendon libre)."], "abstract_en": "The hamstring muscles comprise the semimembranosus, semitendinosus, and biceps femoris. The hamstring muscle group is frequently injured in a wide range of sports, particularly those involving sprinting, rapid acceleration and deceleration, and changes of direction. The most common mechanism is an indirect injury, typically presenting as a muscle strain. In elite athletes, hamstring injuries can be career-threatening. Complete hamstring avulsions are less common than partial tears. This review provides an overview of the broad spectrum of sports-related hamstring injuries and their magnetic resonance imaging findings. Furthermore, both classic and more recent classification systems for muscle injuries are presented and discussed, as several systems have been proposed and currently no universally accepted standard exists. In addition, basic principles of treatment, the healing process of muscle injuries, and the risk of re-injury are outlined. Factors influencing the time to return to play are also discussed. Since radiologists play a key role in the multidisciplinary care of both athletes and the general population with muscle injuries, communication with referring physicians should be as standardized as possible to have the same language and based on clinically aligned terminology.", "tags": ["IRM", "Sémiologie", "Classification", "Biceps fémoral", "Skeletal Radiol 2026"], "pdf_file": null, "pdf_source": "external_link", "pdf_size_kb": null, "pubmed_url": "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/42530617/", "doi_url": "https://doi.org/10.1007/s00256-026-05319-8", "pmc_url": "", "article_url": "https://doi.org/10.1007/s00256-026-05319-8"}, {"id": "pmid-42310826", "pmid": "42310826", "pmcid": "", "doi": "10.1177/03635465261455046", "title": "ACL Reconstruction With Attachment-Sparing Hamstring Autograft Results in Earlier Graft Maturation and Better Short-Term Clinical Outcome in Comparison to Free Graft: A Randomized Controlled Trial", "title_fr": "Reconstruction du LCA par autogreffe d'ischio-jambiers préservant l'insertion tibiale : maturation IRM accélérée (Essai randomisé)", "authors_list": ["Sinha S", "Arumugham KC", "Singh DK", "Kumar N", "Bera AP", "Uppal H", "Vakharia KS"], "authors_short": "Sinha S, Arumugham KC et al.", "journal": "The American journal of sports medicine", "journal_short": "Am J Sports Med", "impact_factor": 4.2, "year": 2026, "month_num": 6, "month_fr": "Juin", "pub_date_fr": "Juin 2026", "volume": "54", "issue": "10", "pages": "2399-2406", "category_id": "acl_graft_harvest", "category_label": "Ischio-Jambiers & Plastie du LCA (Prélèvement & Régénération)", "category_short": "Greffe DIDT/DT4 & Régénération", "category_color": "#e11d48", "modality": "Plastie LCA & IRM", "intervention_type": "Greffe DIDT Pédiculée (Attachment-Sparing)", "cochin_link": "Essai contrôlé randomisé de Niveau I (AJSM 2026) prouvant que conserver l'insertion distale de la patte d'oie lors du prélèvement des ischio-jambiers accélère significativement la ligamentisation IRM (score de Figueroa 5/5 chez 100 % des patients à 9 mois vs 10 % en greffe libre).", "summary_fr_short": "Essai randomisé (56 patients, IRM séquentielles à 3, 6 et 9 mois) comparant la plastie du LCA par ischio-jambiers pédiculés sur le tibia (AS) vs greffe libre (FG). Le maintien de l'insertion tibiale évite l'œdème de nécrose avasculaire du greffon et permet une maturation IRM complète chez 100 % des patients à 9 mois.", "summary_fr_detailed": "Lors du prélèvement classique du semi-tendineux et du gracilis au stripper pour reconstruction du LCA, le détachement tibial dévascularise totalement le transplant. Dans cet essai randomisé, la préservation de l'attache tibiale native maintient un apport vasculaire et neuro-proprioceptif : dès 3 mois, 26,3 % des greffons pédiculés présentent un score IRM de Figueroa ≥ 4 (contre 0 % à 3 mois pour les greffes libres), et à 9 mois 100 % atteignent le grade maximal de maturation (score 5) sans gonflement œdémateux du greffon.", "protocol_fr": "Prélèvement des tendons semi-tendineux et gracilis au stripper ouvert en conservant intacte leur insertion distale sur la patte d'oie • Passage dans les tunnels tibial et fémoral • Contrôle IRM séquentiel (score de Figueroa).", "key_points_fr": ["Essai randomisé de Niveau I avec suivi IRM longitudinal à 3, 6 et 9 mois.", "100 % de maturation IRM optimale (score de Figueroa 5) à 9 mois dans le groupe pédiculé contre 10 % en greffe libre.", "Absence de phase de gonflement œdémateux ('graft swelling') du transplant lorsque l'insertion tibiale est préservée."], "abstract_en": "Background Attachment-sparing anterior cruciate ligament reconstruction (ACLR) is gaining acceptance over the use of free grafts because of reported early graft ligamentization in both animals and humans. However, the literature regarding clinical outcomes and the correlation of early graft healing is unclear. Hypothesis Tibial attachment-sparing hamstring graft (AS) results in earlier graft maturation, which correlates with a better clinical outcome than a free hamstring graft (FG). Study design Randomized controlled trial; Level of evidence, 1. Methods A total of 56 patients were randomly assigned to 2 groups at the commencement of the study. Of these patients, 25 (AS group) underwent ACLR using the attachment-sparing technique, while 25 patients (FG group) underwent ACLR using the free hamstring graft technique. Prospective sequential magnetic resonance imaging (MRI) evaluations were performed at 3, 6, and 9 months to document graft maturation, characteristics, orientation, and tunnel dilation. Clinical evaluation was done at 3, 6, 9, and 12 months to document Lysholm, International Knee Documentation Committee (IKDC), and Tegner scores and anterior tibial translation (ATT) by KT-2000 arthrometer, by independent observers. Results The overall follow-up rate was 78% (19 cases and 20 controls of 25 in each group). MRI Figueroa scores demonstrated significant differences between the AS and FG groups, with the AS group showing consistently higher scores at all timelines. The earliest noticed graft maturation in the case group was at 3 months (26.3% had Figueroa score 4), compared to 6 months for the controls. In the AS group, all participants attained a Figueroa score of 5 at the final assessment, whereas only 10% of the FG group achieved that. The AS group maintained nearly the same graft diameters, but the FG group showed increased diameter (graft swelling) at all follow-up time points. A significant increase in tibial tunnel diameter over time was observed in both groups, with a greater increase in the AS group. A significant improvement was observed in Lysholm score, IKDC score, and ATT in both groups. Conclusion Tibial attachment-sparing hamstring grafts result in earlier and complete graft maturation on MRI as compared to free hamstring grafts for ACLR. Registration Clinical Trials Registry - India (CTRI/2022/10/046620).", "tags": ["Plastie LCA", "DIDT pédiculé", "Ligamentisation IRM", "Score de Figueroa", "AJSM 2026"], "pdf_file": null, "pdf_source": "external_link", "pdf_size_kb": null, "pubmed_url": "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/42310826/", "doi_url": "https://doi.org/10.1177/03635465261455046", "pmc_url": "", "article_url": "https://doi.org/10.1177/03635465261455046"}, {"id": "pmid-42281221", "pmid": "42281221", "pmcid": "", "doi": "10.1177/03635465261453077", "title": "Partial Tenotomy and Reattachment for Chronic Proximal Hamstring Tendinopathy: Functional Outcomes, Return to Sport, and Patient Satisfaction", "title_fr": "Ténotomie partielle et réinsertion pour tendinopathie proximale chronique des ischio-jambiers (PHT) : résultats fonctionnels et retour au sport", "authors_list": ["Lefèvre N", "Moussa MK", "Valentin E", "Hadjam T", "Gerometta A", "Grimaud O", "Meyer A", "Bohu Y", "Khiami F", "Hardy A"], "authors_short": "Lefèvre N, Moussa MK et al.", "journal": "The American journal of sports medicine", "journal_short": "Am J Sports Med", "impact_factor": 4.2, "year": 2026, "month_num": 6, "month_fr": "Juin", "pub_date_fr": "Juin 2026", "volume": "54", "issue": "9", "pages": "2213-2219", "category_id": "pht_endoscopy_sciatic", "category_label": "Tendinopathie Proximale (PHT), Endoscopie & Nerf Sciatique", "category_short": "Tendinopathie, Endoscopie & Sciatique", "category_color": "#ec4899", "modality": "Chirurgie & Tendinopathie", "intervention_type": "Ténotomie Partielle & Réinsertion", "cochin_link": "Nouvelle publication 2026 dans l'AJSM de l'équipe du Dr Nicolas Lefèvre validant la chirurgie de ténotomie-réinsertion sur ancres dans les tendinopathies proximales chroniques réfractaires (> 6 mois).", "summary_fr_short": "Sur 42 patients (âge moyen 50,4 ans, suivi moyen 4,1 ans) opérés d'une tendinopathie proximale chronique (PHT) résistante à > 6 mois de traitement médical, la ténotomie partielle avec réinsertion sur ancres fait passer le score PHAS de 63,0 à 84,2 (p < 0,001).", "summary_fr_detailed": "La tendinopathie proximale chronique des ischio-jambiers (PHT), caractérisée par des douleurs ischiatiques basses en position assise et à la course sans rupture franche, peut devenir rebelle à la rééducation excentrique, aux ondes de choc et aux infiltrations de PRP. Cette étude de cohorte évalue l'excision des zones de tendinose dégénérative (ténotomie partielle) associée à une réinsertion trans-osseuse sur ancres et neurolyse sciatique à la demande. À plus de 4 ans de recul moyen (2 à 12,6 ans), le score PHAS progresse de 21,2 points (63,0 à 84,2) avec un taux élevé de retour au sport et de satisfaction.", "protocol_fr": "Indication : PHT symptomatique réfractaire > 6 mois • Abord sous-fessier court • Excision du tissu tendineux nécrotique/fissuraire et avivement de la tubérosité ischiatique • Réinsertion solide par ancres de suture.", "key_points_fr": ["Série de 42 patients suivis en moyenne 4,1 ans (jusqu'à 12,6 ans).", "Amélioration hautement significative du score PHAS moyen de 63,0 ± 11,4 à 84,2 ± 18,2.", "Alternative chirurgicale fiable et efficace lorsque les traitements conservateurs (excentrique, PRP, ondes de choc) échouent après 6 mois."], "abstract_en": "Background Chronic proximal hamstring tendinopathy (PHT) can cause significant pain and functional impairment, particularly in active individuals. While nonoperative management is the first-line treatment, some patients with persistent symptoms may require surgical intervention. Purpose To evaluate the functional outcomes, return-to-sport rates, and complication profiles of patients undergoing partial tenotomy and reinsertion for chronic PHT. Study design Cohort study; Level of evidence, 3. Methods This retrospective cohort study targeted patients who underwent partial tenotomy and reattachment of the proximal hamstring tendons between April 2010 and September 2022. The indication for surgery was refractory chronic PHT symptoms for >6 months. The primary outcome measure was the Parisian Hamstring Avulsion Score (PHAS). Secondary outcomes included the Tegner Activity Scale score, University of California-Los Angeles (UCLA) Activity Scale score, return to sport, postoperative patient satisfaction, and the rate of complications. In this study, the terms \"partial involvement\" or \"complete involvement\" refer to the extent of magnetic resonance imaging signal changes across the tendon, rather than to true tendon ruptures or avulsions. Results The study included 42 patients with a mean age of 50.4 years (SD, 10.4 years). The mean follow-up was 4.1 years (range, 2.0-12.6 years). The PHAS score significantly improved from a preoperative mean of 63.0 (SD, 11.4) to 84.2 (SD, 18.2) at the final follow-up ( P P P Conclusion Partial tenotomy and reattachment for chronic PHT result in significant functional recovery, a high return-to-sport rate, and good patient satisfaction.", "tags": ["Tendinopathie proximale (PHT)", "Ténotomie-réinsertion", "Cohorte PHAS", "Douleur assise", "AJSM 2026"], "pdf_file": null, "pdf_source": "external_link", "pdf_size_kb": null, "pubmed_url": "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/42281221/", "doi_url": "https://doi.org/10.1177/03635465261453077", "pmc_url": "", "article_url": "https://doi.org/10.1177/03635465261453077"}, {"id": "pmid-42358117", "pmid": "42358117", "pmcid": "PMC13736296", "doi": "10.1177/03635465261456228", "title": "Early Versus Delayed Repair of Proximal Hamstring Tendon Ruptures: A Systematic Review and Meta-analysis", "title_fr": "Réparation précoce vs différée des ruptures proximales des ischio-jambiers : revue systématique et méta-analyse (2026)", "authors_list": ["Akhtar M", "Ramanis M", "Pasko K", "Wang D"], "authors_short": "Akhtar M, Ramanis M et al.", "journal": "The American journal of sports medicine", "journal_short": "Am J Sports Med", "impact_factor": 4.2, "year": 2026, "month_num": 6, "month_fr": "Juin", "pub_date_fr": "Juin 2026", "volume": "54", "issue": "13", "pages": "3337-3345", "category_id": "proximal_acute", "category_label": "Ruptures Proximales Aiguës & Cohorte PHAS", "category_short": "Avulsions Proximales & PHAS", "category_color": "#f97316", "modality": "Méta-analyse", "intervention_type": "Chirurgie Précoce (< 4–6 sem.)", "cochin_link": "Confirme à l'échelle internationale (1 720 patients) les conclusions de la cohorte PHAS : opérer dans les 4 à 6 premières semaines divise par deux le risque de re-rupture et par quatre le risque de sciatalgie.", "summary_fr_short": "Méta-analyse incluant 12 études (992 réparations précoces ≤ 4–6 semaines vs 728 réparations différées). La prise en charge précoce réduit significativement le risque de sciatalgie post-opératoire (RR = 0,26 ; p = 0,009) et de re-rupture (RR = 0,51 ; p = 0,03).", "summary_fr_detailed": "Cette méta-analyse publiée dans l'AJSM en 2026 compare les résultats des réparations primaires précoces (≤ 4 à 6 semaines) et différées (> 4 à 6 semaines) hors allogreffes, sur un total de 1 720 patients. Si les scores fonctionnels globaux restent satisfaisants dans les deux groupes, l'intervention précoce diminue de 74 % le risque de souffrance ou radiculalgie sciatique post-opératoire (RR = 0,26) grâce à l'absence de fibrose péri-nerveuse, et diminue de 49 % le taux de re-rupture tendineuse (RR = 0,51).", "protocol_fr": "Indication chirurgicale posée dès l'IRM initiale • Intervention idéale dans les 4 semaines suivant le traumatisme avant constitution de la fibrose péri-sciatique.", "key_points_fr": ["Analyse poolée de 1 720 réparations primaires (992 précoces vs 728 différées).", "Réduction de 74 % du risque de sciatalgie post-opératoire en cas de chirurgie précoce (RR = 0,26).", "Réduction de moitié du taux de re-rupture tendineuse (RR = 0,51 ; p = 0,03).", "Plaide pour un circuit IRM + avis chirurgical spécialisé en urgence différée dès la suspicion clinique."], "abstract_en": "Background Early recognition and repair of proximal hamstring tendon ruptures is critical, as delays can complicate surgical repair. However, the influence of surgical timing on patient outcomes remains unclear, with previous studies reporting variable results. Purpose To evaluate the effect of early versus delayed repair on clinical outcomes after primary proximal hamstring tendon repair. Study design Meta-analysis. Methods A search was performed in accordance with the PRISMA (Preferred Reporting Items for Systematic Reviews and Meta-Analyses) guidelines in the PubMed, Embase, and Scopus databases. Studies were included if they (1) reported outcomes of primary proximal hamstring tendon repair, (2) had ≥12 months of follow-up, (3) included patients ≥18 years, and (4) compared early versus delayed treatment. Studies were excluded if they examined outcomes of proximal hamstring tendon repair/reconstruction using an allograft. Early repair was defined as surgery within 4 to 6 weeks of injury. Risk ratios (RRs) were calculated using a random-effects model, with significance set at P Results Twelve studies were included, encompassing 992 early and 728 delayed repairs. The mean patient age ranged from 29.7 to 53.8 years in the early group and from 29.2 to 51.8 years in the delayed group. The mean follow-up ranged from 24 to 58.8 months. Nine studies reported patient-reported outcomes (PROs) at the latest follow-up; 3 demonstrated ≥1 significantly higher PRO scores in the early repair group. Return-to-sport/activity was reported in 5 studies, but was heterogeneous: 1 study reported a significantly higher rate of sports cessation after delayed repair, whereas others showed no difference in return-to-sport metrics. Early repair was associated with a significantly reduced risk of postoperative sciatica (RR, 0.26; P = .009) and retear (RR, 0.51; P = .03). Risk of peri-incisional numbness (RR, 0.48; P = .37), infection (RR, 0.51; P = .43), and revision (RR, 0.63; P = .36) were not significantly different between early and delayed repair groups. Conclusion Patients undergoing proximal hamstring tendon repair within 4 to 6 weeks of injury can expect mostly similar PROs and a significantly lower risk of retear and sciatica compared with patients undergoing repair after 4 to 6 weeks of injury.", "tags": ["Méta-analyse", "Délai chirurgical", "Re-rupture", "Nerf sciatique", "AJSM 2026"], "pdf_file": null, "pdf_source": "external_link", "pdf_size_kb": null, "pubmed_url": "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/42358117/", "doi_url": "https://doi.org/10.1177/03635465261456228", "pmc_url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC13736296/", "article_url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC13736296/"}, {"id": "pmid-42324766", "pmid": "42324766", "pmcid": "", "doi": "10.1177/03635465261452818", "title": "Two-Year Outcomes of the Proximal Hamstring Avulsion Rehabilitation Regimes: Longitudinal Versus Accelerated Protocol (PHARRLAP) Study: A Randomized Controlled Trial", "title_fr": "Essai randomisé PHARRLAP à 2 ans : Rééducation accélérée sans attelle et appui immédiat vs Protocole conservateur après réinsertion proximale", "authors_list": ["Ebert JR", "Edwards PK", "Cecchi S", "Malik SS", "Aujla RS", "Ricciardo B", "Annear P", "D'Alessandro PA"], "authors_short": "Ebert JR, Edwards PK et al.", "journal": "The American journal of sports medicine", "journal_short": "Am J Sports Med", "impact_factor": 4.2, "year": 2026, "month_num": 6, "month_fr": "Juin", "pub_date_fr": "Juin 2026", "volume": "54", "issue": "9", "pages": "2204-2212", "category_id": "scores_rehab_prevention", "category_label": "Scores PHAS / Hip-RSI, Rééducation Excentrique & RTS", "category_short": "Scores PHAS/RSI, Nordic & RTS", "category_color": "#10b981", "modality": "Essai Randomisé (Niveau I)", "intervention_type": "Rééducation Post-Opératoire", "cochin_link": "Essai contrôlé randomisé de niveau I (AJSM 2026) démontrant que l'appui complet immédiat selon tolérance sans attelle de genou donne des résultats fonctionnels et isocinétiques équivalents à 2 ans sans augmenter les re-ruptures.", "summary_fr_short": "Essai randomisé comparant 27 patients sous protocole accéléré (sans attelle, appui complet immédiat selon tolérance) et 30 patients sous protocole restreint (6 semaines d'attelle et décharge partielle) après réparation aiguë d'au moins 2 tendons : résultats cliniques, isocinétiques et taux de re-rupture identiques à 2 ans.", "summary_fr_detailed": "L'immobilisation post-opératoire par attelle articulée de genou ou de hanche est souvent mal tolérée par les patients. L'essai randomisé PHARRLAP montre que chez des patients opérés en aigu d'une avulsion d'au moins 2 tendons, le groupe sans attelle avec appui immédiat toléré présente moins de douleurs à 3 et 12 mois, un meilleur score physique SF-12 à 3 mois, et une symétrie de force isocinétique des fléchisseurs (LSI PKFT) et de saut monopodal strictement équivalente à 6, 12 et 24 mois (2 re-ruptures dans chaque groupe sur 2 ans).", "protocol_fr": "Protocole accéléré (AR) : absence d'attelle de genou, marche en appui complet protégé par cannes selon confort dès J1, éviction des flexions de tronc jambes tendues pendant 6 semaines, renforcement progressif dès S6.", "key_points_fr": ["Étude randomisée contrôlée de Niveau I avec suivi clinique et isocinétique complet à 3, 6, 12 et 24 mois.", "Moins de douleurs à 3 et 12 mois dans le groupe sans attelle avec remise en charge selon tolérance.", "Récupération identique du couple de force isocinétique des fléchisseurs et du hop-test à 2 ans.", "Pas d'augmentation du taux de re-rupture précoce en l'absence d'attelle rigide de genou."], "abstract_en": "Background Significant variation has been reported within published postoperative rehabilitation protocols after proximal hamstring tendon repair undertaken for acute tendon avulsion injuries, particularly with respect to bracing and the restriction and progression of weightbearing. Purpose To investigate clinical outcomes, the recovery of strength and functional symmetry, and complications and reinjuries in patients undergoing proximal hamstring repair for acute tendon avulsions involving at least 2 tendons, following a conservative (CR) or accelerated (AR) rehabilitation pathway. Study design Randomized controlled trial; Level of evidence, 1. Methods This prospective RCT allocated patients undergoing proximal hamstring tendon repair to the CR pathway (n = 30), which included 6 weeks of knee bracing and restricted weightbearing, or the AR pathway (n = 27), which did not require knee bracing and allowed full weightbearing as tolerated. All patients underwent repair due to an acute avulsion injury involving at least 2 tendons and due to a specific traumatic incident. Patients were assessed presurgery and at 3-, 6-, 12-, and 24-month postoperatively using patient-reported outcome measures (PROMs), peak isokinetic knee flexor (PKFT) and extensor (PKET) torque, and the single-leg horizontal hop test for distance (SLHD). Normalized outcomes and limb symmetry indices (LSIs) were presented for objective measures. Complications and reinjuries were reported. The primary study outcome was the 6-month postoperative LSI for PKFT. Results While the AR group reported a significantly better 12-item Short-Form Health Survey physical score at 3 months ( P = .039) and lower severity of hamstring pain at 3 months ( P = .034) and 12 months ( P = .010), no other group-based differences were observed. The normalized PKFT ( P P P P P P > .05) were observed for hop or strength outcomes. Four patients were reinjured over the 24 months, including 2 CR patients (at 6 weeks and 3 months) and 2 AR patients (at 10 and 12 months), with an overall rerupture rate of 7%. Conclusion After proximal hamstring repair undertaken due to an acute avulsion injury involving at least 2 tendons, patients demonstrated improved PROMs, a sound recovery of strength and function, and a low reinjury rate over a 2-year postoperative period. The AR pathway that did not employ bracing and permitted full weightbearing as tolerated demonstrated subjective, functional, and reinjury outcomes that were largely comparable over the 2 years. However, the study was not powered to investigate differences in reinjury risk. Registration Australian New Zealand Clinical Trials Registry (ACTRN12621000913875).", "tags": ["Essai randomisé (RCT)", "Rééducation post-opératoire", "Attelle", "Isocinétisme", "AJSM 2026"], "pdf_file": null, "pdf_source": "external_link", "pdf_size_kb": null, "pubmed_url": "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/42324766/", "doi_url": "https://doi.org/10.1177/03635465261452818", "pmc_url": "", "article_url": "https://doi.org/10.1177/03635465261452818"}, {"id": "pmid-42144919", "pmid": "42144919", "pmcid": "", "doi": "10.1002/arj.70209", "title": "Endoscopic Proximal Hamstring Repairs Remain Safe and Beneficial With High Patient Satisfaction at a Minimum of 6-Year Follow-Up", "title_fr": "Réparation endoscopique des ruptures proximales des ischio-jambiers : sécurité et résultats maintenus à plus de 6 ans de recul minimum", "authors_list": ["Greenwood SN", "Adebayo T", "Reinke EK", "Fletcher AN", "Lewis BD", "Scott EJ", "Mather RC"], "authors_short": "Greenwood SN, Adebayo T et al.", "journal": "Arthroscopy : the journal of arthroscopic & related surgery : official publication of the Arthroscopy Association of North America and the International Arthroscopy Association", "journal_short": "Arthroscopy", "impact_factor": 4.4, "year": 2026, "month_num": 5, "month_fr": "Mai", "pub_date_fr": "Mai 2026", "volume": "42", "issue": "9", "pages": "1644-1650", "category_id": "pht_endoscopy_sciatic", "category_label": "Tendinopathie Proximale (PHT), Endoscopie & Nerf Sciatique", "category_short": "Tendinopathie, Endoscopie & Sciatique", "category_color": "#ec4899", "modality": "Chirurgie Endoscopique", "intervention_type": "Réinsertion Endoscopique", "cochin_link": "Confirme la durabilité à long terme (suivi moyen 7,6 ans, min 6,2 ans) de la réparation endoscopique pour les lésions partielles ou complètes peu rétractées.", "summary_fr_short": "Suivi à moyen/long terme (6,2 à 10,6 ans, moyenne 7,6 ans) de réparations endoscopiques proximales des ischio-jambiers : score HOS-Sports moyen de 89,2/100, EVA douleur à 1,7/10, 86,4 % de satisfaction et 85,7 % des patients atteignant le seuil PASS.", "summary_fr_detailed": "La chirurgie endoscopique de l'espace sous-fessier profond permet de visualiser magnifiquement la tubérosité ischiatique, de réaliser une neurolyse endoscopique prudente du nerf sciatique et de réinsérer par ancres les déchirures partielles de haut grade ou les avulsions complètes non rétractées. À 7,6 ans de recul moyen, les scores fonctionnels (mHHS 82,6 ; HOS 83,2 ; HOS-SSS 89,2 ; SANE 80,2 %) démontrent le maintien pérenne du bénéfice clinique avec une morbidité cicatricielle minimale.", "protocol_fr": "Installation en décubitus ventral • Deux ou trois voies d'abord dans le pli sous-fessier • Bursectomie ischiatique, repérage et protection du nerf sciatique en latéral • Avivement osseux et réparation trans-tendineuse par ancres sous contrôle endoscopique.", "key_points_fr": ["Plus long suivi publié (moyenne 7,6 ans, jusqu'à 10,6 ans) pour la réparation endoscopique proximale.", "86,4 % de satisfaction globale et 85,7 % des patients atteignant un état symptomatique acceptable (PASS).", "Indication privilégiée : ruptures partielles de haut grade, tendinopathies fissuraires et avulsions aiguës peu rétractées (< 2–3 cm)."], "abstract_en": "Purpose To evaluate midterm outcomes of endoscopic proximal hamstring repair in a previously established cohort. Methods A retrospective study design was used for a midterm (minimum 6 years) follow-up time. Patients completed the questionnaires over the phone or electronically on REDCap after consent. The questionnaires included the modified Harris Hip Score, the Hip Outcome (HOS), the Sports Subscale (HOS-SSS), the Single Assessment Numeric Evaluation, the International Hip Outcome Tool-12, VAS pain scale, and queried on satisfaction with the surgery. Descriptive statistics including mean and variance were used to analyze the results and compare values preoperatively and at the midterm time point. Results Twenty two of 29 individuals participated (75.8%). Minimum follow-up time was 6.2 years with a maximum follow-up time of 10.6 years (mean 7.6, SD 1.08). Subjects reported a mean modified Harris Hip Score of 82.6 (SD 17.1, range 46.2-100.1), mean HOS of 83.2 (SD 19.9, range 33.8-100), mean HOS-SSS of 89.2 (SD 15, range 50-100, n = 12), mean Single Assessment Numeric Evaluation of 80.2% (SD 20.6, range 30-100%), mean the International Hip Outcome Tool-12 of 79.5 (SD 22.7, range 32.5-100), mean pain score of 1.7 (SD 2.4, range 0-7), and 86.4% satisfaction with the surgery. Conclusions Endoscopic hamstring repair remains beneficial with high patient satisfaction (86.4%, n = 22) and patient-reported outcome scores (85.7% reaching acceptable symptom state, n = 21) at midterm follow-up. Level of evidence Level IV, retrospective case series.", "tags": ["Endoscopie", "Espace sous-fessier", "PASS", "Réinsertion par ancres", "Arthroscopy 2026"], "pdf_file": null, "pdf_source": "external_link", "pdf_size_kb": null, "pubmed_url": "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/42144919/", "doi_url": "https://doi.org/10.1002/arj.70209", "pmc_url": "", "article_url": "https://doi.org/10.1002/arj.70209"}, {"id": "pmid-42169801", "pmid": "42169801", "pmcid": "PMC13187365", "doi": "10.1177/23259671251405420", "title": "Development and Validation of a Short Version of the Parisian Hamstring Avulsion Score (PHAS): The Short PHAS", "title_fr": "Développement et validation de la version courte du Parisian Hamstring Avulsion Score : le Short-PHAS (12 items)", "authors_list": ["Kassab Hassan S", "Rougereau G", "Moussa M", "Khiami F", "Gerometta A", "Lefevre N", "Hardy A"], "authors_short": "Kassab Hassan S, Rougereau G et al.", "journal": "Orthopaedic journal of sports medicine", "journal_short": "Orthop J Sports Med", "impact_factor": 2.6, "year": 2026, "month_num": 5, "month_fr": "Mai", "pub_date_fr": "Mai 2026", "volume": "14", "issue": "5", "pages": "23259671251405420", "category_id": "scores_rehab_prevention", "category_label": "Scores PHAS / Hip-RSI, Rééducation Excentrique & RTS", "category_short": "Scores PHAS/RSI, Nordic & RTS", "category_color": "#10b981", "modality": "Score Clinique (PROM)", "intervention_type": "Validation du Short-PHAS", "cochin_link": "Validation sur 335 patients opérés (Clinique du Sport / Cochin / Pitié, 2026) du *Short-PHAS* en 12 items : corrélation de 0,94 avec le PHAS long et prédiction fiable du retour au sport au niveau antérieur (seuil 47/65).", "summary_fr_short": "Sur une cohorte prospective de 335 patients opérés d'une avulsion proximale, le score Short-PHAS (réduit de 36 à 12 items, sur 65 points) démontre une excellente cohérence interne (α = 0,85), une corrélation de r = 0,94 avec le PHAS complet et une bonne valeur prédictive du retour au sport (AUC = 0,75).", "summary_fr_detailed": "Pour faciliter le suivi clinique quotidien en consultation et par télé-suivi, l'équipe parisienne a développé une version condensée en 12 items du Parisian Hamstring Avulsion Score (notée sur 65 points). Validé sur 335 patients (âge moyen 48 ± 10 ans), le Short-PHAS conserve les mêmes performances psychométriques et prédictives que le score long de 36 items : un score seuil de 39/65 prédit la reprise sportive globale, 46/65 la reprise du même sport, et 47/65 le retour au niveau sportif pré-lésionnel (AUC = 0,75).", "protocol_fr": "Auto-questionnaire rapide en 12 items (sur 65 points) administré en pré-opératoire puis à 4, 8 et 12 mois • Seuil cible ≥ 47/65 pour autoriser le retour à la compétition au niveau antérieur.", "key_points_fr": ["Réduction de 36 à 12 items permettant un remplissage ultra-rapide en consultation ou sur smartphone.", "Corrélation quasi-parfaite avec le score PHAS original (r = 0,94 ; Cronbach α = 0,85) sur 335 patients.", "Seuils décisionnels validés sur 65 points : 39/65 (RTS global), 46/65 (même sport), 47/65 (niveau pré-lésionnel)."], "abstract_en": "Background The Parisian Hamstring Avulsion Score (PHAS) was recently validated for the assessment of proximal hamstring avulsion repair. Purpose To validate a short version of the PHAS score: the short PHAS. Study design Cohort study (Diagnosis); Level of evidence, 3. Methods All patients who underwent proximal hamstring tendon avulsion repair between January 2020 and November 2023 were included. The tear was confirmed by preoperative magnetic resonance imaging. Proximal hamstring avulsion was repaired by surgical reinsertion with suture anchors. The long PHAS score, short PHAS score, and return to sport (RTS) were evaluated. The psychometric properties of the short PHAS, including its predictive value for RTS, were evaluated. Results A prospective study was performed. A total of 335 patients were included in the final cohort, with a mean age of 48 ± 10 years. A subgroup of 30 patients was created to develop the short PHAS. A short PHAS score of 12 items (compared with 36 for the long version) was obtained. The internal consistency of the short version was excellent (Cronbach α = 0.85). The short PHAS was strongly correlated with the long PHAS ( r = 0.94). The predictive value of both versions was moderate to good for RTS at 8 months. The predictive values of both the short and long PHAS are acceptable for RTS (area under the curve [AUC] = 0.75 for the short and 0.74 for the long PHAS), with a cutoff value of 39 of 65. Both the short and the long PHAS scores have a moderate predictive value for return to the same sport (AUC = 0.69 short vs 0.69 long PHAS), with a cut-off value of 46 of 65. Both the long and short PHAS scores have an acceptable predictive value for the return to the same sport at the preinjury level (AUC = 0.75 short vs 0.74 long PHAS), with a cutoff value of 47 of 65. Conclusion The short PHAS is a reliable and valid tool to assess RTS after proximal hamstring avulsion repair. Registration NCT02906865.", "tags": ["Short-PHAS", "Score clinique", "Retour au sport", "Validation psychométrique", "OJSM 2026"], "pdf_file": null, "pdf_source": "external_link", "pdf_size_kb": null, "pubmed_url": "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/42169801/", "doi_url": "https://doi.org/10.1177/23259671251405420", "pmc_url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC13187365/", "article_url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC13187365/"}, {"id": "pmid-41918719", "pmid": "41918719", "pmcid": "PMC13033848", "doi": "10.1177/23259671261418679", "title": "Risk Factors for Complications After Proximal Hamstring Avulsion Injury: Analysis of 757 Patients From the PHAS Cohort", "title_fr": "Facteurs de risque de complications après chirurgie d'avulsion proximale des ischio-jambiers : analyse de 757 patients de la cohorte PHAS", "authors_list": ["Lefèvre N", "Moussa MK", "Valentin E", "Freiha K", "Bohu Y", "Gerometta A", "Khiami F", "Grimaud O", "Meyer A", "Hardy A"], "authors_short": "Lefèvre N, Moussa MK et al.", "journal": "Orthopaedic journal of sports medicine", "journal_short": "Orthop J Sports Med", "impact_factor": 2.6, "year": 2026, "month_num": 3, "month_fr": "Mars", "pub_date_fr": "Mars 2026", "volume": "14", "issue": "3", "pages": "23259671261418679", "category_id": "proximal_acute", "category_label": "Ruptures Proximales Aiguës & Cohorte PHAS", "category_short": "Avulsions Proximales & PHAS", "category_color": "#f97316", "modality": "Chirurgie & Clinique", "intervention_type": "Réinsertion Proximal (Cohorte PHAS)", "cochin_link": "Étude de référence de la cohorte prospective PHAS (Dr Nicolas Lefèvre, Clinique du Sport Paris, n = 757) établissant la cartographie exacte des complications post-opératoires.", "summary_fr_short": "Sur 757 patients opérés d'une avulsion proximale des ischio-jambiers (2002–2023), le taux global de complications est de 10,4 %, comprenant 5,6 % de re-ruptures et 4,7 % de complications hors re-rupture. Trois quarts des complications surviennent dans les 5,5 premiers mois post-opératoires.", "summary_fr_detailed": "Cette large cohorte prospective (757 patients, âge moyen 47,3 ans) analyse les complications après réinsertion primaire des avulsions proximales des ischio-jambiers (PHAI). Le taux de complications hors re-rupture est faible (4,7 %), mais impacte significativement le score PHAS final et le retour au sport au niveau antérieur (31,3 % vs 65,0 %, p = 0,0137). Les deux facteurs de risque indépendants de complications hors re-rupture sont le contexte d'accident du travail (OR = 5,59) et l'atteinte isolée du tendon semi-membraneux (OR = 12,1). Une gêne assise modérée à sévère persiste chez seulement 5 % des patients.", "protocol_fr": "Réinsertion anatomique trans-osseuse sur la tubérosité ischiatique par ancres de suture • Suivi prospectif standardisé par scores PHAS, Tegner et UCLA (NCT02906865).", "key_points_fr": ["Taux global de complications de 10,4 % sur 757 patients : 5,6 % de re-ruptures et 4,7 % d'autres complications.", "75 % des événements indésirables surviennent au cours des 5,5 premiers mois post-opératoires.", "L'accident du travail (OR = 5,59) et l'avulsion isolée du semi-membraneux (OR = 12,1) augmentent le risque de complications.", "La douleur en position assise prolongée ne concerne qu'une minorité de patients (~5 %) à long terme."], "abstract_en": "Background Proximal hamstring avulsion injuries (PHAIs) are significant but rare injuries that might require surgical repair. However, limited data exist on postoperative complication rates, associated risk factors, and long-term musculoskeletal outcomes. Purpose To analyze the complication rate following PHAI surgery, identify risk factors for non-rerupture complications, and investigate the incidence of associated musculoskeletal conditions. Study design Cohort study; Level of evidence, 3. Methods This prospective cohort study included 757 patients (mean age, 47.3 years) surgically treated for PHAI between 2002 and 2023. Patients with revision surgery or bony avulsions were excluded. Primary outcomes were complication rates and associated risk factors. Secondary outcomes included the functional outcomes of patients with or without complications, assessed through the Parisian Hamstring Avulsion Score (PHAS), Tegner Activity Scale, and UCLA score (University of California, Los Angeles). Results The overall complication rate was 10.4% (79/757), including a 5.6% rerupture rate and 4.7% non-rerupture complication rate. Most complications (75%) occurred within 5.5 months postsurgery. Significant risk factors for non-rerupture complications included work-related accidents (odds ratio, 5.59; 95% CI, 1.43-37.0; P = .029) and isolated semimembranosus injuries (odds ratio, 12.1; 95% CI, 2.13-227; P = .020). Moderate to severe sitting discomfort was reported in 5% of the cohort but was not associated with postoperative complications. Functional outcomes were worse in the complication group, with lower PHAS ( P = .0053) and reduced return-to-sport rates at the preoperative level (31.3% vs 65.0%; P = .0137). Conclusion PHAI surgery has a 4.7% complication rate excluding reruptures and 10.4% including them. These complications notably impair functional outcomes. Work-related accidents and isolated semimembranosus injuries are risk factors for non-rerupture complications. Moderate to severe sitting discomfort occurs in a minority of patients (~5%) and does not appear directly linked to postoperative complications. Registration NCT02906865 (ClinicalTrials.gov identifier).", "tags": ["Avulsion proximale", "Complications", "Cohorte PHAS", "Semi-membraneux", "OJSM 2026"], "pdf_file": null, "pdf_source": "external_link", "pdf_size_kb": null, "pubmed_url": "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/41918719/", "doi_url": "https://doi.org/10.1177/23259671261418679", "pmc_url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC13033848/", "article_url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC13033848/"}, {"id": "pmid-41685043", "pmid": "41685043", "pmcid": "PMC12891400", "doi": "10.1177/23259671251413602", "title": "Long-term Outcomes and Return to Sport After Surgical Repair of Proximal Hamstring Avulsions: A ≥8-Year Follow-up Study", "title_fr": "Résultats à très long terme (≥ 8 ans) et retour au sport après réinsertion chirurgicale des avulsions proximales des ischio-jambiers", "authors_list": ["Lefèvre N", "Moussa MK", "Nouira Z", "Valentin E", "Bohu Y", "Gerometta A", "Khiami F", "Meyer A", "Grimaud O", "Hardy A"], "authors_short": "Lefèvre N, Moussa MK et al.", "journal": "Orthopaedic journal of sports medicine", "journal_short": "Orthop J Sports Med", "impact_factor": 2.6, "year": 2026, "month_num": 2, "month_fr": "Févr.", "pub_date_fr": "Févr. 2026", "volume": "14", "issue": "2", "pages": "23259671251413602", "category_id": "proximal_acute", "category_label": "Ruptures Proximales Aiguës & Cohorte PHAS", "category_short": "Avulsions Proximales & PHAS", "category_color": "#f97316", "modality": "Chirurgie & Suivi ≥ 8 ans", "intervention_type": "Réinsertion par Ancres", "cochin_link": "Démontre la pérennité des résultats fonctionnels au-delà de 10 ans de recul moyen (123,4 mois) dans la cohorte parisienne PHAS et l'importance cruciale d'opérer en phase aiguë (< 4 semaines).", "summary_fr_short": "Sur 139 patients suivis en moyenne plus de 10 ans (123,4 mois, minimum 8 ans), le score PHAS atteint 87,5/100 et le score UCLA passe de 3,1 à 7,5. Le score PHAS continue de progresser favorablement jusqu'à 8 ans post-opératoires.", "summary_fr_detailed": "Étude prospective de niveau II évaluant 139 patients (âge moyen 44,4 ans) opérés entre 2002 et 2015 avec un recul minimum de 8 ans (moyenne 10,3 ans). Les résultats fonctionnels sont excellents et durables (PHAS moyen 87,5 ± 14,9). Point fondamental : l'analyse longitudinale montre que le score PHAS continue de s'améliorer au fil des années jusqu'à 8 ans après la chirurgie. En revanche, les réparations réalisées au stade chronique (> 4 semaines) présentent un risque près de 6 fois plus élevé de mauvais résultat fonctionnel à long terme comparativement aux réparations aiguës.", "protocol_fr": "Réinsertion chirurgicale primaire par ancres sur la tubérosité ischiatique • Évaluation longitudinale à 1, 2, 4, 6 et ≥ 8 ans par scores PHAS, UCLA et Tegner.", "key_points_fr": ["Recul moyen exceptionnel de 123,4 mois (> 10 ans) sur 139 patients opérés.", "Score PHAS moyen à long terme de 87,5/100 et amélioration majeure du score UCLA (de 3,1 à 7,5).", "La récupération fonctionnelle (PHAS) poursuit sa progression jusqu'à 8 ans post-opératoires.", "La chirurgie retardée (> 4 semaines) multiplie par ~6 le risque de score PHAS inférieur à long terme."], "abstract_en": "Background Proximal hamstring avulsion injuries (PHAIs) are debilitating injuries that can affect long-term quality of life. Purpose To evaluate the long-term functional results of PHAI repair and determine the rate of patient satisfaction and the quality of their return to sport at the latest follow-up. Study design Prospective cohort study; Level of evidence, 2. Methods This was a prospective cohort study conducted from January 2002 to May 2015. The study targeted all patients undergoing PHAI repair during this time frame, ensuring a minimum follow-up of 8 years. The primary outcome measure used was the Parisian Hamstring Avulsion Score (PHAS). Secondary outcome measures included the University of California at Los Angeles (UCLA) activity scale score and the Tegner activity scale score. A satisfaction survey was administered, the complication rate was assessed, and both the rate and quality of return to sport were analyzed. A subanalysis comparing acute ( 4 weeks) repairs was performed. Results The study involved 139 patients with a mean age of 44.4 years (SD, 11.3 years) and a mean follow-up duration of 123.4 months (SD, 30.8 months). The PHAS at the last follow-up was 87.5 (SD, 14.9). This score was significantly affected by follow-up length (ANOVA-type statistic = 65.40497; df = 4.050724; P P = .04 and P = .001, respectively) and at 4 years compared with 6 and 8 years ( P = .001 and P = .01, respectively). The UCLA activity scale score improved significantly from a preoperative mean of 3.1 (SD, 1.3) to 7.5 (SD, 2.5) at the final follow-up ( P P P Conclusion Surgical repair of PHAI is associated with improved long-term functional outcomes. The PHAS is expected to increase over time until 8 years postoperatively. Chronic repairs carry nearly 6 times higher odds of poor long-term PHAS performance compared with acute repairs.", "tags": ["Long terme", "Avulsion proximale", "Cohorte PHAS", "Retour au sport", "OJSM 2026"], "pdf_file": null, "pdf_source": "external_link", "pdf_size_kb": null, "pubmed_url": "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/41685043/", "doi_url": "https://doi.org/10.1177/23259671251413602", "pmc_url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12891400/", "article_url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12891400/"}, {"id": "pmid-41306750", "pmid": "41306750", "pmcid": "PMC12644451", "doi": "10.1177/23259671251389118", "title": "The Hip-RSI Score for Evaluating Psychological Readiness to Return to Sport After Surgical Repair of a Proximal Hamstring Avulsion", "title_fr": "Validation du score Hip-RSI pour évaluer l'aptitude psychologique au retour au sport après chirurgie d'avulsion proximale des ischio-jambiers", "authors_list": ["Hadjam T", "Lacombe-Ossó J", "Valentin E", "Moussa MK", "Lefèvre N", "Hardy A"], "authors_short": "Hadjam T, Lacombe-Ossó J et al.", "journal": "Orthopaedic journal of sports medicine", "journal_short": "Orthop J Sports Med", "impact_factor": 2.6, "year": 2025, "month_num": 11, "month_fr": "Nov.", "pub_date_fr": "Nov. 2025", "volume": "13", "issue": "11", "pages": "23259671251389118", "category_id": "scores_rehab_prevention", "category_label": "Scores PHAS / Hip-RSI, Rééducation Excentrique & RTS", "category_short": "Scores PHAS/RSI, Nordic & RTS", "category_color": "#10b981", "modality": "Score Psychologique (RSI)", "intervention_type": "Évaluation Psychologique RTS", "cochin_link": "Transpose aux ischio-jambiers l'approche psychologique que le Dr Nicolas Lefèvre avait popularisée avec l'ACL-RSI pour le genou : un score Hip-RSI > 51,7 à 4 mois prédit le retour au sport à 9 mois.", "summary_fr_short": "Étude prospective sur 84 patients opérés d'une avulsion proximale évaluant le score psychologique Hip-RSI à 4 et 9 mois. Fortement corrélé au PHAS (r = 0,55 ; ICC = 0,89), un seuil de Hip-RSI ≥ 51,7/100 à 4 mois prédit le retour au sport antérieur à 9 mois (AUC = 0,76).", "summary_fr_detailed": "L'appréhension de la rechute (kinésiophobie) lors du sprint ou de la flexion de tronc est un frein majeur au retour au sport après réinsertion proximale des ischio-jambiers. Cette étude valide l'utilisation du score Hip-RSI (Return to Sport after Injury) chez 84 patients actifs. Le score présente une excellente reproductibilité (ICC = 0,89) et une bonne capacité discriminante (AUC = 0,76) : dès le 4e mois post-opératoire, un score Hip-RSI supérieur à 51,7 prédit le retour au sport pré-lésionnel à 9 mois avec une spécificité de 77 %.", "protocol_fr": "Passation conjointe du Short-PHAS et du Hip-RSI à 4 mois et 9 mois post-opératoires • Accompagnement mental et réathlétisation progressive si Hip-RSI < 52 à 4 mois.", "key_points_fr": ["Première validation d'un score psychologique de préparation au retour au sport (Hip-RSI) pour les ischio-jambiers.", "Excellente fiabilité test-retest (ICC = 0,89) et corrélation significative avec le score fonctionnel PHAS (r = 0,55).", "Seuil prédictif à 4 mois : Hip-RSI ≥ 51,7/100 (sensibilité 69 %, spécificité 77 %, AUC = 0,76 pour le RTS à 9 mois)."], "abstract_en": "Background The Hip-Return to Sport after Injury (Hip-RSI) score emphasizes the importance of psychological factors in return to sport (RTS) after arthroscopic treatment of femoroacetabular impingement. Purpose/hypothesis The goal of this study was to evaluate the reproducibility and validity of the Hip-RSI score for the assessment of the psychological readiness to RTS after surgical repair of a proximal hamstring avulsion in active patients, and the ability of the score to predict the return to preinjury sport at 9 months postoperatively. It was hypothesized that the Hip-RSI score is a reliable and valid tool for effectively assessing psychological readiness and predicting return to preinjury sport at 9 months after surgery. Study design Cohort study (diagnosis); Level of evidence, 3. Methods Patients who underwent primary surgical repair of a proximal hamstring avulsion between June 2022 and November 2023 were included in this prospective cohort study. All patients completed the Hip-RSI score at 4 and 9 months postoperatively. The Hip-RSI score was compared with the Parisian Hamstring Avulsion Score (PHAS) for validation using the Pearson correlation coefficient. The discriminant and predictive abilities of the Hip-RSI score were also estimated. A test-retest method was used to assess the reliability of the scores, and internal consistency was evaluated using the Cronbach alpha coefficient. Results A total of 84 patients (38 women and 46 men), with a mean age of 48.7 ± 12.7 years (range, 21-85 years), were included in the study. The Hip-RSI score was strongly correlated with the PHAS score ( r = 0.55 [95% CI, 0.37-0.68]). The intraclass correlation coefficient was 0.89 (95% CI, 0.75-0.96), indicating good reliability of the score over time. The discriminant ability of the Hip-RSI score was good, as it was significantly higher in the RTS group than in patients who did not return to their preinjury sport. Furthermore, the predictive ability to identify patients who would return to their preinjury sport at 9 months postoperatively was acceptable (area under the curve = 0.76). At an optimal cutoff value of 51.7, the Hip-RSI score at 4 months had a sensitivity of 69% and a specificity of 77%, with a moderate Youden index of 0.46. The internal consistency suggested redundancy among the score items (alpha coefficient = .97). Conclusion The Hip-RSI score is a valid, reproducible tool to assess the psychological readiness to RTS in active patients who undergo surgical repair of a proximal hamstring avulsion. This score, with a cutoff of 51.7 at 4 months postoperatively, demonstrated a moderate ability to predict which patients would return to sport, with a sensitivity of 69% and a specificity of 77%.", "tags": ["Hip-RSI", "Psychologie du sport", "Kinésiophobie", "Cohorte PHAS", "OJSM 2025"], "pdf_file": null, "pdf_source": "external_link", "pdf_size_kb": null, "pubmed_url": "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/41306750/", "doi_url": "https://doi.org/10.1177/23259671251389118", "pmc_url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12644451/", "article_url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12644451/"}, {"id": "pmid-40985316", "pmid": "40985316", "pmcid": "", "doi": "10.1111/sms.70136", "title": "Clinical Presentation and Rehabilitation Progression Following Hamstring Injury Assessed by BAMIC in Elite Track and Field", "title_fr": "Présentation clinique et progression de la rééducation des lésions des ischio-jambiers selon la classification BAMIC chez l'athlète d'élite", "authors_list": ["Giakoumis M", "Kelly S", "Lee J", "Stone B", "Polglass G", "Brown J", "MacDonald B", "Pollock N"], "authors_short": "Giakoumis M, Kelly S et al.", "journal": "Scandinavian journal of medicine & science in sports", "journal_short": "Scand J Med Sci Sports", "impact_factor": 4.1, "year": 2025, "month_num": 10, "month_fr": "Oct.", "pub_date_fr": "Oct. 2025", "volume": "35", "issue": "10", "pages": "e70136", "category_id": "imaging_bamic_mri", "category_label": "Imagerie IRM, Signe du Cobra, Tendon Central & BAMIC", "category_short": "IRM, BAMIC & Signe du Cobra", "category_color": "#6366f1", "modality": "IRM & Clinique (BAMIC)", "intervention_type": "Classification BAMIC (Type c)", "cochin_link": "Établit les corrélations clinico-radiologiques des lésions du tendon intramusculaire (BAMIC type 'c') chez les athlètes d'élite : douleur plus élevée à la marche et délai prolongé avant reprise du footing indolore.", "summary_fr_short": "Chez l'athlète d'athlétisme d'élite, les lésions touchant le tendon central/intramusculaire (BAMIC classe 'c') entraînent une EVA douleur supérieure à la marche et à la contraction résistée, ainsi qu'un allongement significatif du délai de retour à l'entraînement complet (TRFT).", "summary_fr_detailed": "Étude prospective menée auprès d'athlètes de haut niveau corrélant l'examen clinique initial, le grade IRM BAMIC (0 à 4, sous-types a myofascial, b jonction myo-tendineuse, c intra-tendineux) et le temps de retour à l'entraînement complet (TRFT). Fait notable : 30 % des athlètes présentaient des lombalgies ou des crampes/raideurs des ischio-jambiers dans la semaine précédant la déchirure. Les lésions intra-tendineuses de classe 'c' mettent significativement plus de temps à retrouver une extension passive indolore du genou et un footing sans douleur. La disparition de la douleur à la palpation (p = 0,69) ne prédit pas le délai de retour au jeu, contrairement au test MHFAKE et au test H d'Askling.", "protocol_fr": "Classification IRM BAMIC systématique (grade 0–4 et site a/b/c) • Surveillance clinique par test MHFAKE (Maximal Hip Flexion Active Knee Extension) et Askling H-test avant autorisation du sprint.", "key_points_fr": ["30 % des ruptures aiguës sont précédées de lombalgies ou de tensions/crampes dans les 7 jours avant l'accident.", "Les lésions du tendon intramusculaire (BAMIC 'c') ont une EVA plus élevée à la marche et un TRFT nettement allongé.", "La douleur à la palpation locale (p = 0,69) n'est pas un bon marqueur pronostique, contrairement au déficit d'amplitude MHFAKE et au test d'Askling."], "abstract_en": "The primary aim was to report clinical features at initial assessment after hamstring injury, and the association of these with the mechanism of injury, BAMIC, and time to return to full training (TRFT). A secondary aim was to monitor functional milestones during rehabilitation and assess these relative to BAMIC and TRFT. Hamstring injuries in elite track and field athletes were eligible for inclusion. Clinical markers and the time to reach functional milestones in rehabilitation and TRFT were recorded. Low back pain and hamstring cramping/tightness in the week prior to acute injury were, independently, present in 30% of injuries. A reduction in Maximal Hip Flexion Active Knee Extension (MHFAKE) (p = 0.04), greater MHFAKE asymmetry (p = 0.01), increased Visual Analogue Scale (VAS) pain on walking (p = 0.001) and resisted hamstring contraction (p = 0.01) were associated with TRFT. BAMIC c injuries had a higher VAS pain score on walking and hamstring contraction, took longer to jog, and for passive knee extension to be pain free (p = 0.04). Time to return to pain-free jogging, pain-free and full power on manual muscle testing, Askling H-test negative, and full passive ROM were associated with TRFT. Pain-free palpation (p = 0.69) was not associated with TRFT. Class c injuries are challenging to detect clinically, but a higher VAS pain score and prolonged restriction in range may be clinical associations. MHFAKE and VAS on walking and hamstring contraction may be helpful in prognostication. Low back pain and hamstring tightness/cramping are common symptoms prior to acute hamstring injury.", "tags": ["BAMIC", "Tendon intramusculaire", "Athlétisme élite", "Askling H-test", "Scand J Med Sci Sports 2025"], "pdf_file": null, "pdf_source": "external_link", "pdf_size_kb": null, "pubmed_url": "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/40985316/", "doi_url": "https://doi.org/10.1111/sms.70136", "pmc_url": "", "article_url": "https://doi.org/10.1111/sms.70136"}, {"id": "pmid-40815844", "pmid": "40815844", "pmcid": "", "doi": "10.1177/03635465251359550", "title": "Outcomes of Revision Versus Primary Repair of Proximal Hamstring Avulsion Injury", "title_fr": "Résultats des reprises chirurgicales (révisions) comparés aux réparations primaires des avulsions proximales des ischio-jambiers", "authors_list": ["Lefèvre N", "Moussa MK", "Orso A", "Valentin E", "Bohu Y", "Gerometta A", "Khiami F", "Grimaud O", "Meyer A", "Hardy A"], "authors_short": "Lefèvre N, Moussa MK et al.", "journal": "The American journal of sports medicine", "journal_short": "Am J Sports Med", "impact_factor": 4.2, "year": 2025, "month_num": 8, "month_fr": "Août", "pub_date_fr": "Août 2025", "volume": "53", "issue": "11", "pages": "2676-2683", "category_id": "chronic_revision_allograft", "category_label": "Ruptures Chroniques, Révisions & Greffes (Allogreffes)", "category_short": "Chroniques, Allogreffes & Révisions", "category_color": "#8b5cf6", "modality": "Chirurgie de Révision", "intervention_type": "Révision vs Chirurgie Primaire", "cochin_link": "Étude cas-témoins appariée 1:4 (32 révisions vs 128 primaires) quantifiant le défi technique des reprises chirurgicales : recours à une allogreffe dans 37,5 % des cas et risque de re-rupture itérative de 31,3 %.", "summary_fr_short": "Comparaison appariée de 32 révisions chirurgicales et 128 réparations primaires (recul ~4 ans). Si le score PHAS médian atteint 85,0 après révision (vs 91,5 en primaire, p = 0,01), le risque de nouvelle rupture est multiplié par 8 (31,3 % vs 3,9 % ; HR = 8,19).", "summary_fr_detailed": "Première étude comparative appariée dédiée aux reprises d'échec de réinsertion proximale des ischio-jambiers. Le recours à une allogreffe de comblement est quatre fois plus fréquent en révision (37,5 % vs 9,4 %, p = 0,0003). Malgré des scores d'activité Tegner et UCLA proches à 4 ans chez les patients consolidés, la chirurgie de révision expose à un taux de re-rupture de 31,3 % (contre 3,9 % en primaire, HR = 8,19) et à une persistance douloureuse plus fréquente (50 % sans douleur ou douleur minime vs 72,4 % en primaire).", "protocol_fr": "Planification pré-opératoire systématique avec allogreffe disponible en salle • Neurolyse extensive du nerf sciatique engainé dans la cicatrice • Rééducation post-opératoire ralentie et protégée.", "key_points_fr": ["Nécessité d'une allogreffe dans 37,5 % des chirurgies de révision (contre 9,4 % en primaire).", "Score PHAS médian honorable après révision (85,0 vs 91,5 en primaire).", "Risque de re-rupture multiplié par 8 en révision (31,3 % vs 3,9 %, HR = 8,19).", "Souligne l'importance capitale de réussir la réparation primaire en phase aiguë (< 32 jours)."], "abstract_en": "Background Proximal hamstring avulsion injury (PHAI) repairs are increasing with the growing frequency of this pathology. That is why rerupture is increasingly encountered with rare studies reporting this outcome. Purpose To compare the functional outcomes of revision versus primary PHAI surgery as measured by the Parisian Hamstring Avulsion Score (PHAS) and to compare other outcomes scores, return-to-sport status, and complication rates. Study design Case-control study; Level of evidence, 4. Methods This single-center, matched case-control study was conducted from 2009 to 2023 at a center specializing in sports surgery. It included adults >18 years of age who underwent revision or primary PHAI repair and had a minimum 1-year follow-up. The study involved matching patients who underwent revision surgery to patients who underwent primary surgery at a 1:4 ratio based on age, sex, Tegner Activity Scale (TAS) score before injury, type of rupture, and follow-up duration. Surgical treatment was indicated for complete PHAI, partial tendon avulsion with >2 cm of retraction, or nonresponse to 6 months of nonoperative treatment. Outcomes measured were the PHAS, complication rate including rerupture, TAS score, UCLA activity score, and patient satisfaction. Results The study included 160 patients, with 128 in the primary group and 32 in the revision group. The mean age for the primary group was 48.6 years (SD, 9.4 years), and for the revision group, it was 48.8 years (SD, 9.4 years); both groups had similar follow-up periods: 45.5 months (SD, 39.5 months) for the primary group and 51.0 months (SD, 46.5 months) for the revision group. Allograft usage was substantially higher in the revision group at 37.5% (12/32) compared with 9.4% (12/128) in the primary group ( P = .0003). At the final follow-up, PHAS values were lower in the revision group with a median of 85.0 (IQR, 64.5-92.0) versus 91.5 (IQR, 78.5-97.0) in the primary group ( P = .01). The rerupture rate also differed significantly, standing at 31.3% (10/32) in the revision group compared with only 3.9% (5/128) in the primary group, with Kaplan-Meier and Cox regression analyses confirming a higher risk of rerupture, quantified as 8.19 (95% CI, 2.76-24.3; P P = .02). TAS and UCLA scores did not show significant differences ( P > .05). Rates of return to sport were similarly comparable across the groups ( P > .05). Pain reported as none or mild affected 72.4% (89/123) of the primary group, but only 50.0% (11/22) of the revision group, marking a significant difference ( P = .01). Conclusion Revision PHAI repair is associated with lower functional scores and nearly 8 times the risk of rerupture compared with primary surgery. Furthermore, it carries a higher rate of complications.", "tags": ["Chirurgie de révision", "Re-rupture", "Allogreffe", "Cohorte PHAS", "AJSM 2025"], "pdf_file": null, "pdf_source": "external_link", "pdf_size_kb": null, "pubmed_url": "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/40815844/", "doi_url": "https://doi.org/10.1177/03635465251359550", "pmc_url": "", "article_url": "https://doi.org/10.1177/03635465251359550"}, {"id": "pmid-41132280", "pmid": "41132280", "pmcid": "PMC12541743", "doi": "10.1016/j.eats.2025.103773", "title": "Reconstruction of Chronic Proximal Hamstring Avulsion Injury Using Quadriceps Allograft: A Technical Note", "title_fr": "Reconstruction des avulsions proximales chroniques des ischio-jambiers par allogreffe de tendon quadricipital : note technique", "authors_list": ["Lefèvre N", "Sezer HB", "Alayane A", "Fares A", "Moussa MK", "Hardy A"], "authors_short": "Lefèvre N, Sezer HB et al.", "journal": "Arthroscopy techniques", "journal_short": "Arthrosc Tech", "impact_factor": 1.3, "year": 2025, "month_num": 8, "month_fr": "Août", "pub_date_fr": "Août 2025", "volume": "14", "issue": "9", "pages": "103773", "category_id": "chronic_revision_allograft", "category_label": "Ruptures Chroniques, Révisions & Greffes (Allogreffes)", "category_short": "Chroniques, Allogreffes & Révisions", "category_color": "#8b5cf6", "modality": "Technique Chirurgicale", "intervention_type": "Allogreffe Quadricipitale", "cochin_link": "Description pas-à-pas de la technique originale du Dr Nicolas Lefèvre utilisant une allogreffe fraîche congelée de tendon quadricipital pour ponter les rétractions chroniques majeures.", "summary_fr_short": "Note technique décrivant l'utilisation d'une allogreffe de tendon quadricipital (avec pastille/extrémité patellaire épaisse) pour reconstruire les ruptures proximales chroniques irréparables sans imposer de flexion forcée du genou.", "summary_fr_detailed": "Dans les avulsions chroniques anciennes avec rétraction sévère (> 8–10 cm), l'allogreffe de tendon quadricipital offre une résistance biomécanique remarquable et une géométrie idéale : son extrémité proximale épaisse permet un ancrage solide par ancres multiples sur la tubérosité ischiatique avivée, tandis que son éventail aponévrotique distal vient envelopper ('wrap') le moignon des tendons ischio-jambiers rétractés, évitant le recours à une attelle en flexion du genou post-opératoire.", "protocol_fr": "Allogreffe fraîche congelée de tendon quadricipital • Ancrage tubérositaire proximal par 3 à 4 ancres • Suture distale circonférentielle en manchon autour du tendon conjoint et du semi-membraneux.", "key_points_fr": ["Géométrie asymétrique idéale : extrémité proximale dense sur l'ischion et éventail distal enveloppant le moignon.", "Évite la mise en flexion forcée du genou lors de la suture et en post-opératoire.", "Excellente résistance biomécanique initiale permettant une rééducation progressive sécurisée."], "abstract_en": "Chronic proximal hamstring ruptures are complex to manage as the result of tendon retraction, scarring, and potential sciatic nerve involvement. When primary repair is not feasible, graft reconstruction becomes necessary. This Technical Note describes a surgical technique using a fresh-frozen quadriceps tendon allograft to bridge chronic, irreparable proximal hamstring tears. The graft offers both sufficient length and biomechanical strength. The thick patellar end allows secure fixation to the ischial tuberosity with multiple anchors, while the fan-shaped distal portion is wrapped around the retracted tendon stump, minimizing the need for excessive knee flexion during fixation.", "tags": ["Note technique", "Allogreffe quadricipitale", "Rupture chronique", "Arthrosc Tech 2025"], "pdf_file": null, "pdf_source": "external_link", "pdf_size_kb": null, "pubmed_url": "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/41132280/", "doi_url": "https://doi.org/10.1016/j.eats.2025.103773", "pmc_url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12541743/", "article_url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12541743/"}, {"id": "pmid-41220667", "pmid": "41220667", "pmcid": "PMC12598220", "doi": "10.1016/j.eats.2025.103844", "title": "Anatomic Reinsertion of Isolated Proximal Semimembranosus Tendon Avulsion in Athletic Population: A Technical Note", "title_fr": "Réinsertion anatomique des avulsions proximales isolées du tendon semi-membraneux chez le sportif : note technique", "authors_list": ["Lefèvre N", "Alayane A", "Moussa MK", "Bohu Y", "Meyer A", "Hardy A"], "authors_short": "Lefèvre N, Alayane A et al.", "journal": "Arthroscopy techniques", "journal_short": "Arthrosc Tech", "impact_factor": 1.3, "year": 2025, "month_num": 8, "month_fr": "Août", "pub_date_fr": "Août 2025", "volume": "14", "issue": "10", "pages": "103844", "category_id": "chronic_revision_allograft", "category_label": "Ruptures Chroniques, Révisions & Greffes (Allogreffes)", "category_short": "Chroniques, Allogreffes & Révisions", "category_color": "#8b5cf6", "modality": "Technique Chirurgicale", "intervention_type": "Réinsertion Semi-Membraneux", "cochin_link": "Détaille l'anatomie chirurgicale de l'empreinte antéro-latérale du semi-membraneux sur l'ischion et la correspondance per-opératoire du « Signe du Cobra ».", "summary_fr_short": "Description étape par étape de la réinsertion anatomique des avulsions isolées du tendon proximal du semi-membraneux chez l'athlète, mettant en évidence l'aspect per-opératoire de l'enroulement distal du moignon (Signe du Cobra).", "summary_fr_detailed": "Bien que plus rare que l'avulsion complète des 3 tendons, l'avulsion isolée du chef proximal du semi-membraneux survient typiquement chez le sportif (danse, arts martiaux, sprint, grand écart) et compromet la reprise sportive en l'absence de chirurgie. L'article décrit le repérage de l'empreinte facettaire supéro-latérale sur la tubérosité ischiatique, le déroulement du moignon tendineux replié sur lui-même ('Cobra sign') qui fait surestimer la rétraction en IRM, et sa fixation anatomique par ancres.", "protocol_fr": "Incision dans le pli sous-fessier • Identification du nerf sciatique en latéral et du tendon conjoint intact en médial • Déroulement du moignon du semi-membraneux (Signe du Cobra) • Réinsertion sur la facette antéro-latérale de l'ischion.", "key_points_fr": ["L'insertion proximale du semi-membraneux est située en antéro-latéral par rapport au tendon conjoint (BF + ST).", "Le déroulement per-opératoire de l'extrémité repliée ('Cobra sign') restitue ~2 cm de longueur tendineuse.", "Permet une réinsertion directe sans tension excessive chez le sportif."], "abstract_en": "Acute hamstring tears are common athletic injuries. Most of these injuries involve muscle belly strain or affect the myotendinous junction. These lesions can often be managed nonoperatively, resulting in a satisfactory functional outcome after an appropriate rehabilitation program. However, acute proximal hamstring avulsion injury occurs less commonly, and early surgical reinsertion is recommended to avoid long-term functional disabilities and to allow a successful return to sport. In this article, we describe a step-by-step surgical technique for reinsertion of proximal isolated semimembranosus tendon avulsion in the athletic population, highlighting the significance of the preoperative radiologic cobra-sign diagnosis and its intraoperative manifestation in this rare injury.", "tags": ["Semi-membraneux isolé", "Signe du Cobra", "Technique chirurgicale", "Arthrosc Tech 2025"], "pdf_file": null, "pdf_source": "external_link", "pdf_size_kb": null, "pubmed_url": "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/41220667/", "doi_url": "https://doi.org/10.1016/j.eats.2025.103844", "pmc_url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12598220/", "article_url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12598220/"}, {"id": "pmid-39844676", "pmid": "39844676", "pmcid": "", "doi": "10.1002/ksa.12596", "title": "Improved outcomes of proximal hamstring avulsion surgery in patients both under and over 50 years, with greater gains in the younger group: A matched comparative study of the PHAS cohort", "title_fr": "Résultats de la chirurgie d'avulsion proximale des ischio-jambiers avant et après 50 ans : étude comparative appariée de la cohorte PHAS", "authors_list": ["Lefèvre N", "Moussa MK", "Alban Bouché P", "Valentin E", "Gerometta A", "Khiami F", "Grimaud O", "Meyer A", "Bohu Y", "Hardy A"], "authors_short": "Lefèvre N, Moussa MK et al.", "journal": "Knee surgery, sports traumatology, arthroscopy : official journal of the ESSKA", "journal_short": "KSSTA", "impact_factor": 3.3, "year": 2025, "month_num": 1, "month_fr": "Janv.", "pub_date_fr": "Janv. 2025", "volume": "33", "issue": "5", "pages": "1853-1862", "category_id": "proximal_acute", "category_label": "Ruptures Proximales Aiguës & Cohorte PHAS", "category_short": "Avulsions Proximales & PHAS", "category_color": "#f97316", "modality": "Chirurgie & Clinique", "intervention_type": "Réinsertion par Ancres", "cochin_link": "Valide l'indication opératoire chez le patient actif de plus de 50 ans : le bénéfice fonctionnel est majeur et le taux de complications n'est pas plus élevé que chez le sujet jeune.", "summary_fr_short": "Étude cas-témoins appariée (298 patients : 18–50 ans vs > 50 ans). La chirurgie procure une amélioration fonctionnelle hautement significative dans les deux tranches d'âge, avec un taux de retour au sport et de complications strictement comparable.", "summary_fr_detailed": "Sur 298 patients appariés selon le sexe, le type de rupture (complète ou partielle rétractée > 2 cm), la chronicité et le score de Tegner pré-lésionnel (âge moyen 41,8 ans vs 58,0 ans, suivi > 4 ans), la réinsertion chirurgicale améliore significativement les scores PHAS, UCLA et Tegner dans les deux groupes. Bien que l'amplitude du gain soit légèrement supérieure chez les moins de 50 ans, les patients de plus de 50 ans obtiennent d'excellents résultats fonctionnels sans sur-risque de complication ni baisse du taux de retour au sport.", "protocol_fr": "Indication chirurgicale maintenue après 50 ans devant toute avulsion complète ou partielle rétractée > 2 cm chez un patient actif.", "key_points_fr": ["298 patients appariés (groupe < 50 ans vs groupe > 50 ans d'âge moyen 58 ans).", "Gain fonctionnel significatif sur le score PHAS dans les deux groupes d'âge.", "Aucune différence significative sur le taux de complications ou le taux de retour au sport (p > 0,05).", "L'âge civil > 50 ans ne doit pas contre-indiquer la réinsertion chirurgicale chez le patient sportif."], "abstract_en": "Purpose To evaluate the functional outcomes of surgical treatment for proximal hamstring avulsion injuries in patients aged over 50 years and to compare the results across another matched group of patients under 50. Methods This was a retrospective analysis of prospectively collected data in a matched case-control design targeting patients with proximal hamstring avulsion injuries who underwent surgical treatment at a sports surgery referral centre. Patients over 50 years with complete avulsion or partial injury (>2 cm retraction) were included. Two age groups were formed (18-50 and over 50), matched by the following variables: gender, rupture type (complete or partial), injury chronicity (chronic: more than 4 weeks from the injury or acute) and the preinjury Tegner score. The primary outcome was the Parisian Hamstring Avulsion Score (PHAS), with secondary outcomes, including the Tegner Activity Scale, University of California, Los Angeles (scale) (UCLA) scale, return to sport and complication rates. Results The study included 298 patients, with a mean age of 41.8 (7.2) years for the younger group and 58.0 (5.7) years for the older group. The follow-up duration in the younger group, 4.7 years (3.2), was slightly longer than that of the older group, 4.1 years (2.5), (p 0.05), with no significant differences in complication and return to sports rates (p > 0.05). Conclusion This study shows that surgical treatment of proximal hamstring avulsion injuries is associated with significant improvements in functional outcomes in both younger and older patients, with greater magnitude of improvements in younger patients. Complication rates were similar between the age groups. Level of evidence Level III, Cohort Study.", "tags": ["Plus de 50 ans", "Cohorte PHAS", "Appariement", "Retour au sport", "KSSTA 2025"], "pdf_file": null, "pdf_source": "external_link", "pdf_size_kb": null, "pubmed_url": "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/39844676/", "doi_url": "https://doi.org/10.1002/ksa.12596", "pmc_url": "", "article_url": "https://doi.org/10.1002/ksa.12596"}, {"id": "pmid-39461588", "pmid": "39461588", "pmcid": "PMC11863337", "doi": "10.1016/j.jshs.2024.100996", "title": "Multiscale hamstring muscle adaptations following 9 weeks of eccentric training", "title_fr": "Adaptations multi-échelles des ischio-jambiers après 9 semaines d'entraînement excentrique (Nordic Hamstring) : ajout de sarcomères en série", "authors_list": ["Andrews MH", "S AP", "Gurchiek RD", "Pincheira PA", "Chaudhari AS", "Hodges PW", "Lichtwark GA", "Delp SL"], "authors_short": "Andrews MH, S AP et al.", "journal": "Journal of sport and health science", "journal_short": "J Sport Health Sci", "impact_factor": 9.7, "year": 2024, "month_num": 10, "month_fr": "Oct.", "pub_date_fr": "Oct. 2024", "volume": "14", "issue": "", "pages": "100996", "category_id": "scores_rehab_prevention", "category_label": "Scores PHAS / Hip-RSI, Rééducation Excentrique & RTS", "category_short": "Scores PHAS/RSI, Nordic & RTS", "category_color": "#10b981", "modality": "Biomécanique & Prévention", "intervention_type": "Nordic Hamstring Exercise (NHE)", "cochin_link": "Preuve directe in vivo chez l'humain (micro-endoscopie par seconde harmonique + IRM, Stanford/Queensland 2025) que l'exercice Nordic Hamstring allonge les fascicules du long biceps de +33 % par addition réelle de sarcomères en série (+49 %).", "summary_fr_short": "Après 9 semaines d'entraînement supervisé en Nordic Hamstring Exercise (NHE), la force excentrique augmente de +40 %, la longueur des fascicules du chef long du biceps fémoral augmente de +33 % en distal, grâce à une addition mesurée de +49 % de sarcomères en série.", "summary_fr_detailed": "Pour la première fois chez l'humain, cette étude combine l'échographie 3D, l'IRM et la micro-endoscopie optique non linéaire pour mesurer directement le nombre de sarcomères en série au sein du chef long du biceps fémoral (BFlh). Neuf semaines de Nordic Hamstring induisent une hypertrophie (+8 % de volume), un gain de force de flexion de +40 %, et une sarcomerogenèse longitudinale massive (+25 % au centre, +49 % en distal). Attention toutefois : un désentraînement de seulement 3 semaines fait reperdre la moitié de ce gain de longueur fasciculaire (-16 %), imposant un maintien continu en saison.", "protocol_fr": "Programme Nordic Hamstring supervisé sur 9 semaines + maintien hebdomadaire obligatoire en saison pour prévenir la réversion rapide (dès 3 semaines d'arrêt) des sarcomères en série.", "key_points_fr": ["Démonstration directe in vivo de la sarcomerogenèse en série (+49 % en région distale du BFlh).", "Allongement fasciculaire de +19 % (zone centrale) à +33 % (zone distale) et +40 % de force excentrique.", "Réversibilité rapide : 3 semaines d'arrêt suffisent à perdre ~16 % de longueur fasciculaire (nécessité d'une dose d'entretien hebdomadaire)."], "abstract_en": "Background Eccentric training, such as Nordic hamstring exercise (NHE) training, is commonly used as a preventive measure for hamstring strains. Eccentric training is believed to induce lengthening of muscle fascicles and to be associated with the addition of sarcomeres in series within muscle fibers. However, the difficulty in measuring sarcomere adaptation in human muscles has severely limited information about the precise mechanisms of adaptation. This study addressed this limitation by measuring the multiscale hamstring muscle adaptations in response to 9 weeks of NHE training and 3 weeks of detraining. Methods Twelve participants completed 9 weeks of supervised NHE training, followed by a 3-week detraining period. We assessed biceps femoris long-head (BFlh) muscle fascicle length, sarcomere length, and serial sarcomere number in the central and distal regions of the muscle. Additionally, we measured muscle volume and eccentric strength at baseline, post-training, and post-detraining. Results NHE training over 9 weeks induced significant architectural and strength adaptations in the BFlh muscle. Fascicle length increased by 19% in the central muscle region and 33% in the distal muscle region. NHE also induced increases in serial sarcomere number (25% in the central region and 49% in the distal region). BFlh muscle volume increased by 8%, and knee flexion strength increased by 40% with training. Following 3 weeks of detraining, fascicle length decreased by 12% in the central region and 16% in the distal region along with reductions in serial sarcomere number. Conclusion Nine weeks of NHE training produced substantial, region-specific increases in BFlh muscle fascicle length, muscle volume, and force generation. The direct measurement of sarcomere lengths revealed that the increased fascicle length was accompanied by the addition of sarcomeres in series within the muscle fascicles.", "tags": ["Nordic Hamstring", "Sarcomères en série", "Biceps fémoral", "Prévention", "J Sport Health Sci 2025"], "pdf_file": null, "pdf_source": "external_link", "pdf_size_kb": null, "pubmed_url": "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/39461588/", "doi_url": "https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jshs.2024.100996", "pmc_url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11863337/", "article_url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11863337/"}, {"id": "pmid-39171445", "pmid": "39171445", "pmcid": "PMC11792104", "doi": "10.1002/ksa.12437", "title": "The Cobra sign: A marker for overestimation of tendon retraction in proximal semimembranosus tendon avulsion", "title_fr": "Le « Signe du Cobra » en IRM : un nouveau marqueur de surestimation de la rétraction tendineuse dans l'avulsion proximale du semi-membraneux", "authors_list": ["Lefèvre N", "Moussa MK", "Chahal A", "Meyer A", "Grimaud O", "Khalaf ZM", "Alayane A", "Bohu Y", "Hardy A"], "authors_short": "Lefèvre N, Moussa MK et al.", "journal": "Knee surgery, sports traumatology, arthroscopy : official journal of the ESSKA", "journal_short": "KSSTA", "impact_factor": 3.3, "year": 2024, "month_num": 8, "month_fr": "Août", "pub_date_fr": "Août 2024", "volume": "33", "issue": "2", "pages": "739-749", "category_id": "imaging_bamic_mri", "category_label": "Imagerie IRM, Signe du Cobra, Tendon Central & BAMIC", "category_short": "IRM, BAMIC & Signe du Cobra", "category_color": "#6366f1", "modality": "IRM & Chirurgie", "intervention_type": "Sémiologie IRM (Signe du Cobra)", "cochin_link": "Découverte sémiologique majeure de l'équipe du Dr Nicolas Lefèvre : l'aspect en tête de cobra du moignon du semi-membraneux entraîne une surestimation radiologique moyenne de 2 cm (21 %) de la rétraction réelle.", "summary_fr_short": "Étude de 81 avulsions proximales opérées définissant le « Signe du Cobra » en IRM (bande hypo-T2 ondulée dont l'extrémité libre se replie distalement sur elle-même). Ce signe a une spécificité de 83,3 % pour le semi-membraneux et surestime la rétraction de 21 % (10,05 cm en IRM vs 7,9 cm en chirurgie).", "summary_fr_detailed": "En IRM sagittale/coronale T2, l'extrémité proximale avulsée du tendon semi-membraneux se replie souvent distalement sur elle-même en prenant la forme d'une tête de cobra dressée. Identifié chez 25 patients sur 81, ce signe présente une spécificité de 83,3 % (LR+ = 3,0) pour une avulsion du semi-membraneux. Surtout, les radiologues mesurent souvent la distance entre l'ischion et le dôme du pli plutôt que l'extrémité réelle repliée : la rétraction mesurée en IRM est de 10,05 ± 3,0 cm alors qu'elle n'est que de 7,9 ± 2,5 cm en per-opératoire après déroulement du tendon (différence moyenne de 2,0 cm, soit 21 % de surestimation).", "protocol_fr": "Recherche systématique du Signe du Cobra sur les coupes sagittales et coronales T2/DP-FS • Ne pas récuser une réinsertion primaire directe sur la seule foi d'une rétraction IRM apparente de 10 cm si le signe du Cobra est présent.", "key_points_fr": ["Définition du Signe du Cobra : repli distal en crosse de l'extrémité libre hypo-T2 du tendon semi-membraneux.", "Spécificité de 83,3 % et rapport de vraisemblance positif de 3,0 pour l'avulsion du semi-membraneux.", "Surestimation moyenne de la rétraction en IRM de 2,0 cm (10,05 cm en IRM contre 7,9 cm réels en chirurgie, soit -21 %).", "Impact chirurgical direct : évite de prévoir à tort une allogreffe alors qu'une réinsertion directe est réalisable une fois le tendon déroulé."], "abstract_en": "Purpose To introduce a new magnetic resonance imaging (MRI) sign, termed the Cobra sign, and identify its diagnostic metrics. The secondary aim was to demonstrate that this sign can be a source of false evaluation of tendon retraction in patients with proximal hamstring avulsion injury. Method This retrospective cohort study targeted patients surgically treated for proximal hamstring avulsion injury from January 2019 to June 2023. The MRI Cobra sign was defined as a wavy curved T2-hypointense band with the free end folding distally over itself, resembling a cobra head. The primary outcome measure was the characterization of the Cobra sign in patients with proximal hamstring avulsion injury. The secondary outcome was the association of this sign with tendon retraction. The study included 81 proximal hamstring avulsion injury patients (mean age of 45.7, SD = 13.9), with 41 (50.6%) complete avulsions, 33 semimembranosus, and 7 conjoint tendons. Results The MRI Cobra sign was found in 25 patients (17 semimembranosus and 8 complete). It was confirmed surgically only in semimembranosus cases. It demonstrated 51.5% sensitivity and 83.3% specificity for isolated semimembranosus avulsions, with a significant positive likelihood ratio of 3.0. MRI retraction was 10.05 cm (±3.0), reducing to 7.9 cm (±2.5) on surgical measurement (mean difference = 2.0 cm, p Conclusion The Cobra sign demonstrated acceptable diagnostic accuracy for isolated semimembranosus avulsion, with a high specificity of 83.3%, a low sensitivity of 51.5%, and a positive likelihood ratio of 3.0. The presence of the Cobra sign indicates an overestimated MRI retraction by approximately 21%. Level of evidence Level III.", "tags": ["Signe du Cobra", "IRM", "Semi-membraneux", "Rétraction tendineuse", "KSSTA 2025"], "pdf_file": null, "pdf_source": "external_link", "pdf_size_kb": null, "pubmed_url": "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/39171445/", "doi_url": "https://doi.org/10.1002/ksa.12437", "pmc_url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11792104/", "article_url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11792104/"}, {"id": "pmid-39165177", "pmid": "39165177", "pmcid": "PMC11402259", "doi": "10.1177/03635465241270139", "title": "Surgical Treatment of Proximal Hamstring Avulsion Injuries Compared With Nonsurgical Treatment: A Matched Comparative Study With a Mean Follow-up of >4 Years From the Proximal Hamstring Avulsion Surgery Cohort Study", "title_fr": "Traitement chirurgical vs traitement conservateur des avulsions proximales des ischio-jambiers : étude comparative appariée à plus de 4 ans de recul", "authors_list": ["Lefèvre N", "Moussa MK", "El Otmani L", "Valentin E", "Meyer A", "Grimaud O", "Bohu Y", "Hardy A"], "authors_short": "Lefèvre N, Moussa MK et al.", "journal": "The American journal of sports medicine", "journal_short": "Am J Sports Med", "impact_factor": 4.2, "year": 2024, "month_num": 8, "month_fr": "Août", "pub_date_fr": "Août 2024", "volume": "52", "issue": "11", "pages": "2718-2727", "category_id": "proximal_acute", "category_label": "Ruptures Proximales Aiguës & Cohorte PHAS", "category_short": "Avulsions Proximales & PHAS", "category_color": "#f97316", "modality": "Chirurgie vs Conservateur", "intervention_type": "Étude Appariée (Score de Propension)", "cochin_link": "Démonstration directe par appariement sur score de propension de la supériorité nette de la chirurgie sur le traitement fonctionnel (satisfaction 89,5 % vs 25 %, retour au niveau antérieur 67,7 % vs 26,7 %).", "summary_fr_short": "Comparaison appariée entre 95 patients opérés et 32 patients ayant refusé la chirurgie (suivi moyen > 4 ans). Le groupe chirurgical obtient un score PHAS nettement supérieur (86,3 vs 69,8), une satisfaction de 89,5 % (vs 25 %) et 6 fois plus de chances de reprendre son sport au même niveau.", "summary_fr_detailed": "Cette étude comparative de la cohorte PHAS utilise un appariement par score de propension pour comparer le traitement chirurgical (n = 95) au traitement non chirurgical chez des patients présentant une indication opératoire mais ayant refusé l'intervention (n = 32). À plus de 4 ans de recul moyen, tous les indicateurs favorisent la chirurgie : PHAS moyen (86,3 vs 69,8), scores Tegner et UCLA (p = 0,0026), retour au sport global (68,4 % vs 46,9 %) et surtout retour au niveau pré-lésionnel (67,7 % vs 26,7 % ; OR = 6,04). Le taux de satisfaction atteint 89,5 % après chirurgie contre seulement 25 % sans chirurgie.", "protocol_fr": "Indication chirurgicale : avulsion complète, ou avulsion partielle rétractée > 2 cm, ou échec à 6 mois du traitement médical.", "key_points_fr": ["Score PHAS à > 4 ans : 86,3 ± 13,7 (chirurgie) contre 69,8 ± 15,1 (traitement non chirurgical).", "Probabilité multipliée par 6 (OR = 6,04) de retrouver son niveau sportif antérieur après chirurgie.", "Taux de satisfaction des patients : 89,5 % dans le groupe opéré contre 25 % dans le groupe non opéré.", "Argument majeur pour éclairer le consentement des patients hésitant devant l'intervention."], "abstract_en": "Background Surgical outcomes for proximal hamstring avulsion injury (PHAI) are well documented, yet comparative analyses with nonsurgical approaches remain scarce. Purpose To compare the functional outcomes between surgical and nonsurgical interventions for PHAI. Study design Cohort study; Level of evidence, 3. Methods This comparative study, conducted at a sports surgery center between January 2012 and July 2021, focused on patients with primary PHAI. The study was a retrospective analysis of prospectively collected data. Group selection involved utilizing propensity score matching to compare an arm of patients who were surgically treated (indications included patients with complete injury, patients with partial injury with >2 cm of retraction, and patients for whom 6 months of nonsurgical treatment failed) with another arm of patients who refused surgery. The primary outcome was evaluated using the Parisian Hamstring Avulsion Score (PHAS). The secondary outcomes included the Tegner Activity Scale (TAS) score; University of California, Los Angeles (UCLA) score; rate and quality of return to sport (RTS); and patient satisfaction. Results The study included 32 patients (mean age, 55.8 years [SD, 8.4 years]) in the nonsurgical treatment arm and 95 patients in the surgical treatment arm (mean age, 53.4 years [SD, 7.7 years]) ( P > .05). The interval from injury to treatment was 5.7 months (SD, 9.6 months) for the surgical group and 12.7 months (SD, 25.9 months) for the nonsurgical group ( P > .05). At the final follow-up (nonsurgical group: mean, 56.5 months [SD, 28.2 months]; surgical group: mean, 50.7 months [SD, 33.1 months]), the PHAS was significantly higher in the surgical group (mean, 86.3 [SD, 13.7]) compared with the nonsurgical group (mean, 69.8 [SD, 15.1]) ( P P = .0224 and P = .0026, respectively). A higher percentage of the surgical group (68.4%) returned to sports compared with the nonsurgical group (46.9%) ( P = .0354), with a greater proportion in the surgical group returning at the same or higher level (67.7% vs 26.7%) ( P = .0069). Additionally, a higher satisfaction level was reported by patients in the surgical group (89.5%) in contrast to the nonsurgical group (25%) ( P P P = .045), and UCLA score (OR, 3.63; P = .003), as well as to RTS at any level (OR, 2.46; P = .031) or at the preinjury level or higher (OR, 6.04; P Conclusion This study demonstrated that surgical treatment of PHAI significantly enhances long-term functional scores, including the PHAS, TAS score, UCLA score, satisfaction, and RTS, at a mean follow-up of >4 years compared with nonsurgical treatment. Registration NCT02906865 (ClinicalTrials.gov identifier).", "tags": ["Chirurgie vs Conservateur", "Score de propension", "Cohorte PHAS", "Satisfaction", "AJSM 2024"], "pdf_file": null, "pdf_source": "external_link", "pdf_size_kb": null, "pubmed_url": "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/39165177/", "doi_url": "https://doi.org/10.1177/03635465241270139", "pmc_url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11402259/", "article_url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11402259/"}, {"id": "pmid-38769849", "pmid": "38769849", "pmcid": "", "doi": "10.1002/ksa.12258", "title": "Magnetic resonance imaging should be the first-line imaging modality for investigating suspected proximal hamstring avulsion injuries", "title_fr": "L'IRM doit être l'examen de première intention devant toute suspicion d'avulsion proximale des ischio-jambiers (vs Échographie)", "authors_list": ["Lefèvre N", "Coughlan A", "Valentin E", "Sezer HB", "Bohu Y", "Moussa MK", "Hardy A"], "authors_short": "Lefèvre N, Coughlan A et al.", "journal": "Knee surgery, sports traumatology, arthroscopy : official journal of the ESSKA", "journal_short": "KSSTA", "impact_factor": 3.3, "year": 2024, "month_num": 5, "month_fr": "Mai", "pub_date_fr": "Mai 2024", "volume": "32", "issue": "7", "pages": "1862-1870", "category_id": "imaging_bamic_mri", "category_label": "Imagerie IRM, Signe du Cobra, Tendon Central & BAMIC", "category_short": "IRM, BAMIC & Signe du Cobra", "category_color": "#6366f1", "modality": "IRM vs Échographie", "intervention_type": "Parcours Diagnostique", "cochin_link": "Démonstration sur 392 patients de la cohorte PHAS que prescrire une échographie en premier retarde la chirurgie de 10 jours (30 j vs 20 j) et dégrade les scores sportifs finaux (Tegner et UCLA).", "summary_fr_short": "Sur 392 patients opérés d'une avulsion proximale, ceux ayant bénéficié d'une IRM d'emblée sont opérés nettement plus tôt (médiane 20 jours vs 30 jours après échographie première) et obtiennent de meilleurs scores d'activité Tegner et UCLA à 4–5 ans.", "summary_fr_detailed": "L'échographie initiale en traumatologie des ischio-jambiers méconnaît fréquemment les avulsions proximales profondes (masquées par l'hématome fessier et l'épaisseur des parties molles) ou sous-estime le nombre de tendons rompus. Dans cette étude de 392 patients (2012–2020), la réalisation d'une IRM en première intention réduit de 10 jours le délai médian jusqu'à la chirurgie (20 jours vs 30 jours, restant ainsi largement sous le seuil critique des 32 jours) et permet des scores fonctionnels Tegner (médiane 5 vs 4) et UCLA significativement supérieurs au dernier recul.", "protocol_fr": "Bannir l'échographie isolée devant un traumatisme proximal sévère de la loge postérieure • Prescrire d'emblée une IRM du bassin/cuisse en urgence différée (< 7–10 jours).", "key_points_fr": ["Analyse comparative de 392 patients : groupe 'IRM d'emblée' vs groupe 'Échographie d'emblée'.", "Gain de 10 jours sur le délai chirurgical médian grâce à l'IRM première (20 jours contre 30 jours).", "Meilleurs résultats sportifs à long terme (scores Tegner et UCLA supérieurs) dans le groupe IRM première.", "Permet d'opérer les patients avant la barrière pronostique des 32 jours."], "abstract_en": "Purpose The purpose of the study was to evaluate how the initial diagnostic tool used-specifically ultrasound (US) or magnetic resonance imaging (MRI)-for diagnosing proximal hamstring avulsion injury affects the delay before surgery and, secondarily, the outcomes of these injuries. Methods This was a retrospective analysis of prospectively collected data (2012-2020). It targeted patients primarily treated for proximal hamstring avulsion injury. It included all patients with a preoperative US and/or MRI. Patients were divided into two groups based on the initial diagnostic tool used (US-first vs. MRI-first groups). The primary outcomes measure was the time from initial injury to surgical intervention (surgical delay). The secondary outcomes were the Parisien Hamstring Avulsion Score, as well as the activity level as measured by the University of California, Los Angeles (UCLA) Activity Scale and Tegner Activity Scale. Results The analysis included 392 patients with a mean age of 43.8 ± 13.6 years for the MRI-first group and 47.6 ± 12.0 years for US-first group. Patients in the MRI-first group had a significantly shorter median time from injury to surgery of 20.0 days (interquartile range [IQR]: 11.0-61.0) compared to 30 days (IQR: 18-74) in the US-first group. At the final follow-up (4.2 ± 2.2 years for the MRI-first group and 5.1 ± 1.9 years for the US-first group), the MRI-first group had significantly higher mean Tegner Activity Scale and UCLA scores than the US-first group: The Tegner Activity Scale was median 5 (IQR: 3-7) for the MRI-first group versus median 4 (IQR: 2-6) for the US-first group (p Conclusion MRI as the initial diagnostic tool for proximal hamstring avulsion injury is associated with a shorter time to surgery and better postoperative outcomes in Tegner Activity Scale and UCLA scores, compared to US. Level of evidence Level III.", "tags": ["IRM première intention", "Échographie", "Délai diagnostique", "Cohorte PHAS", "KSSTA 2024"], "pdf_file": null, "pdf_source": "external_link", "pdf_size_kb": null, "pubmed_url": "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/38769849/", "doi_url": "https://doi.org/10.1002/ksa.12258", "pmc_url": "", "article_url": "https://doi.org/10.1002/ksa.12258"}, {"id": "pmid-38482843", "pmid": "38482843", "pmcid": "PMC10986149", "doi": "10.1177/03635465241233734", "title": "Risk Factors for Rerupture After Proximal Hamstring Avulsion Injury Including the Optimal Timing for Surgery", "title_fr": "Facteurs de risque de re-rupture après avulsion proximale des ischio-jambiers et détermination du délai chirurgical optimal (Seuil des 32 jours)", "authors_list": ["Lefèvre N", "Freiha K", "Moussa MK", "Valentin E", "Bohu Y", "Meyer A", "Hardy A"], "authors_short": "Lefèvre N, Freiha K et al.", "journal": "The American journal of sports medicine", "journal_short": "Am J Sports Med", "impact_factor": 4.2, "year": 2024, "month_num": 3, "month_fr": "Mars", "pub_date_fr": "Mars 2024", "volume": "52", "issue": "5", "pages": "1173-1182", "category_id": "proximal_acute", "category_label": "Ruptures Proximales Aiguës & Cohorte PHAS", "category_short": "Avulsions Proximales & PHAS", "category_color": "#f97316", "modality": "Chirurgie & Pronostic", "intervention_type": "Timing Chirurgical Optimal", "cochin_link": "Article princeps sur 740 patients définissant le seuil critique de 32 jours au-delà duquel le risque de re-rupture est multiplié par 2,5.", "summary_fr_short": "Sur 740 patients opérés (suivi moyen 4,9 ans), le taux de re-rupture est de 4,59 % (34/740), survenant dans 75 % des cas durant les 6 premiers mois (médiane 88,5 jours) et de façon atraumatique (74 %). Un délai avant chirurgie > 32 jours multiplie par 2,5 le risque de re-rupture.", "summary_fr_detailed": "L'analyse multivariée et par courbe ROC de 740 réparations primaires de la cohorte PHAS identifie précisément le seuil temporel définissant la chronicité à risque : 32 jours post-traumatiques. Dépasser un délai de 32 jours entre l'accident et la chirurgie augmente significativement le risque de re-rupture (HR = 2,5 ; IC 95 % 1,24–5,06 ; p = 0,01), tout comme le caractère initialement complet de l'avulsion des 3 tendons (HR = 4,33 ; p = 0,04). Trois quarts des re-ruptures surviennent avant 6 mois (médiane à 3 mois) souvent sans nouveau traumatisme violent.", "protocol_fr": "Objectif organisationnel : réaliser l'IRM diagnostique et la réinsertion chirurgicale dans les 32 jours suivant le traumatisme initial • Protection prudente de la réinsertion pendant les 3 à 6 premiers mois.", "key_points_fr": ["Taux global de re-rupture primaire de 4,59 % (34/740 patients).", "Seuil optimal par courbe ROC : un délai accident–chirurgie > 32 jours multiplie par 2,5 le risque de re-rupture.", "Les avulsions complètes initiales présentent un HR de re-rupture de 4,33 par rapport aux avulsions partielles.", "75 % des re-ruptures surviennent dans les 6 premiers mois (médiane : 88,5 jours), dont 74 % sans traumatisme franc."], "abstract_en": "Background Despite the prevalence of proximal hamstring avulsion injuries (PHAIs), the understanding of rerupture risk factors and the influence of injury chronicity on these rates remain limited. Purpose To investigate the rerupture rate after PHAI repair and identify its associated risk factors and the optimal time to primary surgery. Study design Case-control study; Level of evidence, 3. Method This is a retrospective analysis of prospectively collected data from the French Proximal Hamstring Avulsion Surgery Cohort Study targeting patients surgically treated for PHAI between 2002 and 2022. The primary outcome measure of this study was the rerupture rate of PHAI repair. The secondary outcome measures included the assessment of the potential risk factors for rerupture as well as the investigation of the incidence rate of rerupture for 100 person-years depending on various injury-surgery delay definitions. Results This study analyzed 740 patients with a mean age of 45.9 years (SD, 13.6 years) and followed up for a mean of 4.9 years (SD, 3.9 years). The rerupture rate was 4.59% (34/740). Most reruptures (75%) occurred within the first 6 months after surgery (median, 88.5 days; interquartile range, 39.5-182 days), and 74% were atraumatic. Univariate analysis identified potential risk factors: longer initial surgery delay (hazard ratio [HR], 1.03; 95% CI, 1.01-1.04; P = .04) and initial complete ruptures (HR, 4.47; 95% CI, 1.07-18.7; P = .04). Receiver operating characteristic curve analysis found the optimal injury-surgery delay cutoff predicting rerupture to be 32 days (area under the curve, 0.62; 95% CI, 0.53-0.71). The relative Youden index was calculated at 0.24, corresponding to a sensitivity of 65% and a specificity of 59%. Surpassing this cutoff showed the highest HR (2.56), narrowest 95% CI (1.27-5.17), and highest incidence of rerupture (1.42 per 100 person-years) ( P = .01). In the multivariate analysis, an injury-surgery delay of >32 days (HR, 2.5; 95% CI, 1.24-5.06; P = .01) and initial complete ruptures (HR, 4.33; 95% CI, 1.04-18.08; P = .04) emerged as significant risk factors for rerupture. Conclusion This study found a 4.59% rerupture risk after PHAI repair. Most reruptures (75%) occurred within the first 6 months after surgery. Risk factors for rerupture included chronicity and initial complete injury. The optimal threshold for chronicity of PHAI lesions, based on rerupture rate, was marked by an injury-surgery delay of >32 days.", "tags": ["Re-rupture", "Délai chirurgical", "Seuil 32 jours", "Cohorte PHAS", "AJSM 2024"], "pdf_file": null, "pdf_source": "external_link", "pdf_size_kb": null, "pubmed_url": "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/38482843/", "doi_url": "https://doi.org/10.1177/03635465241233734", "pmc_url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10986149/", "article_url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10986149/"}, {"id": "pmid-38353018", "pmid": "38353018", "pmcid": "PMC10943609", "doi": "10.1177/03635465231225486", "title": "Predictors of Early Return to Sport After Surgical Repair of Proximal Hamstring Complex Injuries in Professional Athletes: A Prospective Study", "title_fr": "Facteurs prédictifs du retour précoce au sport chez l'athlète professionnel opéré d'une lésion proximale du complexe ischio-jambier", "authors_list": ["Lefèvre N", "Moussa MK", "Valentin E", "Meyer A", "Bohu Y", "Gerometta A", "Khiami F", "Grimaud O", "Khalaf Z", "Hardy A"], "authors_short": "Lefèvre N, Moussa MK et al.", "journal": "The American journal of sports medicine", "journal_short": "Am J Sports Med", "impact_factor": 4.2, "year": 2024, "month_num": 2, "month_fr": "Févr.", "pub_date_fr": "Févr. 2024", "volume": "52", "issue": "4", "pages": "1005-1013", "category_id": "proximal_acute", "category_label": "Ruptures Proximales Aiguës & Cohorte PHAS", "category_short": "Avulsions Proximales & PHAS", "category_color": "#f97316", "modality": "Sportif Professionnel", "intervention_type": "Chirurgie & Retour au Jeu (RTS)", "cochin_link": "Série prospective de 64 athlètes professionnels opérés à Paris : 98,4 % de retour au sport et distinction sémiologique clé entre avulsion osseuse/empreinte vide ('dropped ice cream sign +') et rupture du tendon libre ('dropped ice cream sign -').", "summary_fr_short": "Chez 64 sportifs professionnels (âge moyen 27,3 ans), le taux de retour au sport est de 98,4 %, dont 78,1 % au même niveau de compétition dans un délai moyen de 6,2 ± 2,5 mois. Les taux de maintien de performance atteignent 100 % au handball/escrime/escalade et 95,2 % au football.", "summary_fr_detailed": "Cette étude prospective distingue deux entités anatomiques au sein du complexe proximal : la véritable avulsion de l'empreinte tubérositaire ('dropped ice cream sign positif') et la rupture complète du tendon libre proximal ('dropped ice cream sign négatif', PHTR). En analyse multivariée, les facteurs prédictifs d'un retour au sport plus rapide sont : le sexe masculin, l'atteinte isolée du semi-membraneux, et la rupture du tendon libre proximal (PHTR, HR = 7,77).", "protocol_fr": "Analyse IRM pré-opératoire de l'empreinte tubérositaire ('dropped ice cream sign') • Réinsertion ou suture anatomique précoce • Rééducation spécifique orientée haute performance avec retour moyen à la compétition à 6,2 mois.", "key_points_fr": ["98,4 % de retour au sport chez 64 athlètes professionnels, à un délai moyen de 6,2 ± 2,5 mois.", "95,2 % des footballeurs professionnels et 100 % des handballeurs/escrimeurs retrouvent leur niveau antérieur.", "Distinction pronostique majeure entre avulsion tubérositaire vraie et rupture du tendon libre proximal (PHTR).", "Le sexe masculin, la lésion isolée du semi-membraneux et le type PHTR prédisent un RTS plus précoce."], "abstract_en": "Background Proximal hamstring complex injury (PHCI) is a common injury among professional athletes, particularly those participating in pivot contact sports. Previous studies have suggested that surgery can be effective in restoring function and allowing athletes to return to sport (RTS), but the factors influencing successful RTS have been less clear. Purpose To assess RTS capabilities after surgical treatment of PHCI in professional athletes and to identify favorable predictors of RTS. Study design Case series; Level of evidence, 4. Methods This study (2002-2022) was conducted on professional athletes who underwent surgical treatment for PHCI at a sports surgery center. The primary outcome of the study was the RTS capability, evaluated based on the rate of athletes' return to their preinjury level of competition, time delay to RTS, and quality of RTS as measured using their level of performance and progression of scores on activity scales such as the Tegner Activity Scale (TAS) and University of California, Los Angeles (UCLA), scale. \"Maintained performance\" was defined as athletes returning to the same preinjury activity level (per the TAS and UCLA scale) and perceiving themselves to have maintained their performance. Secondary outcomes covered the potential RTS predictors and complication rate. The study distinguished 2 types of PHCI: proximal hamstring tendon avulsion injury (proximal rupture with empty footprint, or having a \"positive dropped ice cream sign\") and complete proximal hamstring free tendon rupture (PHTR; proximal rupture without empty footprint, or having a \"negative dropped ice cream sign\"). Results The study examined 64 professional athletes (mean age, 27.3 years; 82.8% male) undergoing surgery for PHCI. The RTS rate was 98.4%, with 78.1% of the athletes returning to their preinjury level of competition at 6.2 months (SD, 2.5 months). Twelve (19%) patients had returned to sport at an inferior level of competition, and 2 (3.1%) were unable to continue in their preinjury sport. Subgroup analysis revealed variation in RTS based on sport type, with the highest rate of return to preinjury performance found in athletes in handball and sports with splits (fencing, squash, and escalade; 100%) and soccer (95.2%). In the univariate analysis, male sex (hazard ratio [HR], 4.05; 95% CI, 1.45-11.3; P = .008), higher preinjury TAS score (HR, 1.27; 95% CI, 1.06-1.52; P = .011), injury involving the semimembranosus (HR, 4.84; 95% CI, 2.31-10.2; P P = .001), and PHTR (HR, 7.77; 95% CI, 3.54-17.0; P P = .047) for male sex, 3.86 (95% CI, 1.78-8.37; P P Conclusion Favorable predictors of early RTS were male sex, isolated semimembranosus injury, and PHTR injuries. Registration NCT02906865 (ClinicalTrials.gov identifier).", "tags": ["Athlète professionnel", "Retour au sport", "Dropped ice cream sign", "Football", "AJSM 2024"], "pdf_file": null, "pdf_source": "external_link", "pdf_size_kb": null, "pubmed_url": "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/38353018/", "doi_url": "https://doi.org/10.1177/03635465231225486", "pmc_url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10943609/", "article_url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10943609/"}, {"id": "pmid-38353118", "pmid": "38353118", "pmcid": "PMC10943598", "doi": "10.1177/03635465241227434", "title": "Validation of the Parisian Hamstring Avulsion Score (PHAS) in the Evaluation and Follow-up of Patients Operated for Proximal Hamstring Avulsion", "title_fr": "Validation du Parisian Hamstring Avulsion Score (PHAS) dans l'évaluation et le suivi des patients opérés d'une avulsion proximale", "authors_list": ["Lefevre N", "Kassab Hassan S", "Valentin E", "Bohu Y", "Gerometta A", "Meyer A", "Grimaud O", "Hardy A"], "authors_short": "Lefevre N, Kassab Hassan S et al.", "journal": "The American journal of sports medicine", "journal_short": "Am J Sports Med", "impact_factor": 4.2, "year": 2024, "month_num": 2, "month_fr": "Févr.", "pub_date_fr": "Févr. 2024", "volume": "52", "issue": "4", "pages": "1014-1021", "category_id": "scores_rehab_prevention", "category_label": "Scores PHAS / Hip-RSI, Rééducation Excentrique & RTS", "category_short": "Scores PHAS/RSI, Nordic & RTS", "category_color": "#10b981", "modality": "Score de Référence (PHAS)", "intervention_type": "Création & Validation du PHAS", "cochin_link": "Article fondateur dans l'AJSM (Dr Nicolas Lefèvre et al., 2024) établissant le score PHAS comme gold standard spécifique d'évaluation fonctionnelle et de prédiction du retour au sport après avulsion proximale.", "summary_fr_short": "Validation prospective sur 156 patients (suivi moyen 69 mois) du Parisian Hamstring Avulsion Score (PHAS). Excellente fiabilité (ICC = 0,96 ; Cronbach α = 0,86 ; absence d'effet plafond). Un score PHAS ≥ 86/100 à 9 mois prédit le retour au sport à 1 an avec une spécificité de 81,4 %.", "summary_fr_detailed": "Avant le PHAS, aucun score validé en langue française ou internationale ne permettait de prédire le retour au sport après chirurgie d'avulsion proximale des ischio-jambiers. Sur 156 patients opérés par ancres et suivis plus de 5,5 ans, le PHAS démontre une reproductibilité remarquable (ICC = 0,96), une variation minimale détectable (MDC) de 12,9 points, et une forte corrélation avec les scores UCLA et Tegner. À 9 mois post-opératoires, une valeur seuil de 86/100 prédit la reprise sportive à 1 et 2 ans (spécificité 81,4 %).", "protocol_fr": "Évaluation par le score PHAS (sur 100 points) à 6, 9, 12 et 24 mois • Cible décisionnelle à 9 mois : PHAS ≥ 86/100.", "key_points_fr": ["Excellentes propriétés psychométriques sur 156 patients : Cronbach α = 0,86 et ICC = 0,96.", "Absence totale d'effet plafond à 2 ans, permettant de discriminer finement les sportifs exigeants.", "Seuil prédictif à 9 mois : PHAS ≥ 86/100 (spécificité de 81,4 % pour le retour au sport à 1 an)."], "abstract_en": "Background No validated score is available for the prediction of return to sport (RTS) after proximal hamstring avulsion (PHA) surgery. Purpose To validate a new assessment tool for patients after PHA surgery: the Parisian Hamstring Avulsion Score (PHAS). Study design Cohort study (Diagnosis); Level of evidence, 3. Methods All patients at our clinic who had surgery for PHA between January 2015 and March 2018 were included in this study. A suspected clinical diagnosis of PHA was confirmed by magnetic resonance imaging. PHA was repaired by surgical reinsertion with suture anchors. Minimum postoperative follow-up was 2 years, and the PHAS, University of California, Los Angeles (UCLA), score, and Tegner score were used. The PHAS is a patient-reported outcome measure, evaluating the effect of PHA injury with 9 items. It was validated by calculating its psychometric properties, and then correlation analysis was performed to determine the relationship between the PHAS, UCLA score, and Tegner score. Cutoff values for the prediction of RTS were determined. Results A prospective case series study was performed. A total of 156 patients were included. Median age (first quartile; third quartile) was 54.2 years (44.7 years; 61.3 years), and the mean ± SD time of the final follow-up was 69 ± 11.6 months. Two years after surgery, 66.7% (n = 104) of patients were able to RTS. A strong correlation was noted between all 3 scores at 1 year postoperatively. Overall internal consistency was high, with a Cronbach alpha coefficient of 0.86. The intraclass correlation coefficient was 0.96, showing excellent reliability. The minimal detectable change was 12.9. No patients reached the maximum score at 2 years. Analysis of the receiver operating characteristic curves of the 3 scores at postoperative 9 months in relation to the RTS at 1 and 2 years showed area under the curve values of >0.7, indicating significant discriminant capacity for the RTS. A PHAS cutoff value of 86 at 9 months for the prediction of RTS at postoperative 1 year had a sensitivity of 65.6% (95% CI, 53.7%-77.5%) and a specificity of 81.4% (95% CI, 69.8%-93%). Conclusion PHAS is a valid and reliable tool for follow-up after PHA surgery. It also offers a simple way to predict RTS.", "tags": ["PHAS", "Score de référence", "Retour au sport", "Validation", "AJSM 2024"], "pdf_file": null, "pdf_source": "external_link", "pdf_size_kb": null, "pubmed_url": "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/38353118/", "doi_url": "https://doi.org/10.1177/03635465241227434", "pmc_url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10943598/", "article_url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10943598/"}, {"id": "pmid-36650035", "pmid": "36650035", "pmcid": "", "doi": "10.1136/bjsports-2021-105371", "title": "London International Consensus and Delphi study on hamstring injuries part 1: classification", "title_fr": "Consensus International de Londres (Partie 1) : Classification des lésions des ischio-jambiers", "authors_list": ["Paton BM", "Court N", "Giakoumis M", "Head P", "Kayani B", "Kelly S", "Kerkhoffs GMMJ", "Moore J", "Moriarty P", "Murphy S", "Plastow R", "Pollock N", "Read P", "Stirling B", "Tulloch L", "van Dyk N", "Wilson MG", "Wood D", "Haddad F"], "authors_short": "Paton BM, Court N et al.", "journal": "British journal of sports medicine", "journal_short": "Br J Sports Med", "impact_factor": 11.8, "year": 2023, "month_num": 1, "month_fr": "Janv.", "pub_date_fr": "Janv. 2023", "volume": "57", "issue": "5", "pages": "254-265", "category_id": "imaging_bamic_mri", "category_label": "Imagerie IRM, Signe du Cobra, Tendon Central & BAMIC", "category_short": "IRM, BAMIC & Signe du Cobra", "category_color": "#6366f1", "modality": "Consensus International", "intervention_type": "Classification BAMIC & IRM", "cochin_link": "Premier volet du Consensus International de Londres (F. Haddad, B. Paton et 112 experts mondiaux, BJSM 2023) consacrant la classification BAMIC comme standard international (utilisée par 58 % des experts).", "summary_fr_short": "Étude Delphi internationale réunissant 112 experts mondiaux des ischio-jambiers. La classification BAMIC (British Athletics Muscle Injury Classification) est la plus utilisée au monde (58 %), devant Munich (12 %) et Barcelone (6 %).", "summary_fr_detailed": "Ce consensus international recommande d'enrichir la classification IRM BAMIC en précisant systématiquement : le muscle individuel atteint (BFlh, BFsh, ST, SM), la zone anatomique exacte (insertion proximale, tendon libre, jonction myo-tendineuse, tendon central intramusculaire, jonction distale en T), le mécanisme lésionnel (sprint vs étirement/overstretch), la perte de tension tendineuse ('loss of tension' / 'gapping') et le niveau d'exigence sportive du patient.", "protocol_fr": "Compte-rendu structuré combinant BAMIC (Grade 0–4 + suffixe a/b/c) + Muscle spécifique + Mesure du gap tendineux et de la perte de tension.", "key_points_fr": ["88,4 % de taux de réponse au second tour Delphi parmi 112 experts internationaux.", "La classification BAMIC est plébiscitée par 58 % des centres experts dans le monde.", "Nécessité d'intégrer la spécificité de chaque chef musculaire (SM vs BFlh vs ST) et le mécanisme lésionnel."], "abstract_en": "Muscle injury classification systems for hamstring injuries have evolved to use anatomy and imaging information to aid management and prognosis. However, classification systems lack reliability and validity data and are not specific to individual hamstring muscles, potentially missing parameters vital for sport-specific and activity-specific decision making. A narrative evidence review was conducted followed by a modified Delphi study to build an international consensus on best-practice decision-making for the classification of hamstring injuries. This comprised a digital information gathering survey to a cohort of 46 international hamstring experts (sports medicine physicians, physiotherapists, surgeons, trainers and sports scientists) who were also invited to a face-to-face consensus group meeting in London . Fifteen of these expert clinicians attended to synthesise and refine statements around the management of hamstring injury. A second digital survey was sent to a wider group of 112 international experts. Acceptance was set at 70% agreement. Rounds 1 and 2 survey response rates were 35/46 (76%) and 99/112 (88.4%) of experts responding. Most commonly, experts used the British Athletics Muscle Injury Classification (BAMIC) (58%), Munich (12%) and Barcelona (6%) classification systems for hamstring injury. Issues identified to advance imaging classifications systems include: detailing individual hamstring muscles, establishing optimal use of imaging in diagnosis and classification, and testing the validity and reliability of classification systems. The most used hamstring injury classification system is the BAMIC. This consensus panel recommends hamstring injury classification systems evolve to integrate imaging and clinical parameters around: individual muscles, injury mechanism, sporting demand, functional criteria and patient-reported outcome measures. More research is needed on surgical referral and effectiveness criteria, and validity and reliability of classification systems to guide management.", "tags": ["Consensus de Londres", "BAMIC", "Classification IRM", "Delphi", "BJSM 2023"], "pdf_file": null, "pdf_source": "external_link", "pdf_size_kb": null, "pubmed_url": "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/36650035/", "doi_url": "https://doi.org/10.1136/bjsports-2021-105371", "pmc_url": "", "article_url": "https://doi.org/10.1136/bjsports-2021-105371"}, {"id": "pmid-36650033", "pmid": "36650033", "pmcid": "", "doi": "10.1136/bjsports-2021-105383", "title": "London International Consensus and Delphi study on hamstring injuries part 2: operative management", "title_fr": "Consensus International de Londres (Partie 2) : Indications et prise en charge chirurgicale des lésions des ischio-jambiers", "authors_list": ["Plastow R", "Kerkhoffs GMMJ", "Wood D", "Paton BM", "Kayani B", "Pollock N", "Court N", "Giakoumis M", "Head P", "Kelly S", "Moore J", "Moriarty P", "Murphy S", "Read P", "Stirling B", "Tulloch L", "van Dyk N", "Wilson M", "Haddad F"], "authors_short": "Plastow R, Kerkhoffs GMMJ et al.", "journal": "British journal of sports medicine", "journal_short": "Br J Sports Med", "impact_factor": 11.8, "year": 2023, "month_num": 1, "month_fr": "Janv.", "pub_date_fr": "Janv. 2023", "volume": "57", "issue": "5", "pages": "266-277", "category_id": "pht_endoscopy_sciatic", "category_label": "Tendinopathie Proximale (PHT), Endoscopie & Nerf Sciatique", "category_short": "Tendinopathie, Endoscopie & Sciatique", "category_color": "#ec4899", "modality": "Consensus Chirurgical", "intervention_type": "Indications Opératoires", "cochin_link": "Second volet du Consensus de Londres (R. Plastow, G. Kerkhoffs, F. Haddad et al., BJSM 2023) fixant les critères formels d'indication chirurgicale avec > 87 % d'accord international.", "summary_fr_short": "Le panel international de 112 experts valide trois indications chirurgicales majeures : le gap tendineux avec perte de tension (87,2 % d'accord), les avulsions osseuses déplacées symptomatiques (72,8 %), et les lésions du tendon libre proximal réfractaires au traitement conservateur (72,2 %).", "summary_fr_detailed": "Ce consensus Delphi international clarifie la prise de décision chirurgicale devant une lésion des ischio-jambiers. Outre les avulsions proximales complètes, les experts soulignent l'importance mécanique de la « perte de tension » ('loss of tension') et de l'écart inter-fragmentaire ('gapping') sur les tendons proximaux, centraux ou distaux chez l'athlète à haute exigence fonctionnelle. Le consensus rejette formellement les infiltrations de corticoïdes dans les lésions tendineuses aiguës.", "protocol_fr": "Critères décisionnels : (1) Anatomie lésionnelle (gap tendineux, perte de tension, nombre de tendons), (2) Exigence sportive du patient, (3) Risque de déficit de force ou de récidive sous traitement conservateur.", "key_points_fr": ["87,2 % d'accord sur l'indication chirurgicale en cas de gap tendineux avec perte de tension du fuseau musculo-tendineux.", "70 % des chirurgiens experts estiment que la réparation chirurgicale réduit significativement le risque de récidive.", "Contre-indication consensuelle aux infiltrations de corticoïdes dans les lésions aiguës des ischio-jambiers."], "abstract_en": "The key indications for surgical repair of hamstring injuries (HSIs) remain unclear in the literature due to a lack of high-level evidence and expert knowledge. The 2020 London International Hamstring Consensus meeting aimed to highlight clear surgical indications and to create a foundation for future research. A literature review was conducted followed by a modified Delphi process, with an international expert panel. Purposive sampling was used with two rounds of online questionnaires and an intermediate round involving a consensus meeting. The initial information gathering (round 1) questionnaire was sent to 46 international experts, which comprised open-ended questions covering decision-making domains in HSI. Thematic analysis of responses outlined key domains, which were evaluated by a smaller international subgroup (n=15) comprising clinical academic sports medicine physicians, physiotherapists and orthopaedic surgeons in a consensus meeting. After group discussion of each domain, a series of consensus statements were prepared, debated and refined. A round 2 questionnaire was sent to 112 international hamstring experts to vote on these statements and determine level of agreement. The consensus threshold was set a priori at 70% agreement. Rounds 1 and 2 survey respondents were 35/46 (76%) and 99/112 (88.4%), respectively. The consensus group agreed that the indications for operative intervention included: gapping at the zone of tendinous injury (87.2% agreement) and loss of tension (70.7%); symptomatic displaced bony avulsions (72.8%); and proximal free tendon injuries with functional compromise refractory to non-operative treatment (72.2%). Other important considerations for operative intervention included: the demands of the athlete/patient and the expected functional outcome (87.1%) based on the anatomy of the injury; the risk of functional loss/performance deficit with non-operative management (72.2%); and the capacity to restore anatomy and function (87.1%). Further research is needed to determine whether surgery can reduce the risk of reinjury as consensus was not reached within the whole group (48.2%) but was agreed by surgeons (70%) in the cohort. The consensus group did not support the use of corticosteroids or endoscopic surgery without further evidence. These guidelines will help standardise treatment of HSIs, specifically the indications and decision-making for surgical intervention.", "tags": ["Consensus de Londres", "Indications chirurgicales", "Perte de tension", "BJSM 2023"], "pdf_file": null, "pdf_source": "external_link", "pdf_size_kb": null, "pubmed_url": "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/36650033/", "doi_url": "https://doi.org/10.1136/bjsports-2021-105383", "pmc_url": "", "article_url": "https://doi.org/10.1136/bjsports-2021-105383"}, {"id": "pmid-36650032", "pmid": "36650032", "pmcid": "", "doi": "10.1136/bjsports-2021-105384", "title": "London International Consensus and Delphi study on hamstring injuries part 3: rehabilitation, running and return to sport", "title_fr": "Consensus International de Londres (Partie 3) : Rééducation, course haute vélocité et critères de retour au sport (RTS)", "authors_list": ["Paton BM", "Read P", "van Dyk N", "Wilson MG", "Pollock N", "Court N", "Giakoumis M", "Head P", "Kayani B", "Kelly S", "Kerkhoffs GMMJ", "Moore J", "Moriarty P", "Murphy S", "Plastow R", "Stirling B", "Tulloch L", "Wood D", "Haddad F"], "authors_short": "Paton BM, Read P et al.", "journal": "British journal of sports medicine", "journal_short": "Br J Sports Med", "impact_factor": 11.8, "year": 2023, "month_num": 1, "month_fr": "Janv.", "pub_date_fr": "Janv. 2023", "volume": "57", "issue": "5", "pages": "278-291", "category_id": "scores_rehab_prevention", "category_label": "Scores PHAS / Hip-RSI, Rééducation Excentrique & RTS", "category_short": "Scores PHAS/RSI, Nordic & RTS", "category_color": "#10b981", "modality": "Consensus Rééducation", "intervention_type": "Protocole Réathlétisation & RTS", "cochin_link": "Troisième volet du Consensus de Londres (BJSM 2023) établissant les règles d'or de la progression rééducative, de l'exposition au sprint et de la validation du retour sur le terrain.", "summary_fr_short": "Consensus international (112 experts) sur la rééducation des ischio-jambiers : individualisation selon le muscle et le mécanisme (89,8 %), intégration de la chaîne cinétique lombo-pelvienne (95 %), et exigence d'un sprint maximal totalement indolore avant retour au jeu (85,5 %).", "summary_fr_detailed": "Les experts internationaux s'accordent sur une progression guidée par la capacité fonctionnelle plutôt que par des délais fixes. Le contrôle de l'antéversion pelvienne et de la chaîne cinétique (tronc, fessiers, triceps sural) fait l'objet d'un consensus massif (95 %). Si une gêne légère contrôlée est tolérée lors de certains exercices de renforcement lent, l'exposition progressive à la course à très haute vitesse (sprint > 90–95 % Vmax) doit impérativement être indolore (85,5 % d'accord) avant de valider le retour à la compétition.", "protocol_fr": "3 phases validées par critères : (1) Protection et mise en charge isométrique/concentrique précoce, (2) Renforcement excentrique allongé (Askling L-protocol / Slider / Diver) + contrôle pelvien, (3) Sprint haute vélocité indolore et situations spécifiques au poste.", "key_points_fr": ["95 % d'accord sur le traitement conjoint de la chaîne cinétique (contrôle lombo-pelvien et antéversion du bassin).", "Combinaison d'exercices à dominante hanche (allongement élevé : Diver, RDL) et à dominante genou (Nordic, Slider).", "Critère absolu avant RTS : réalisation de sprints répétés à vitesse maximale strictement indolores (85,5 % d'accord)."], "abstract_en": "Hamstring injuries (HSIs) are the most common athletic injury in running and pivoting sports, but despite large amounts of research, injury rates have not declined in the last 2 decades. HSI often recur and many areas are lacking evidence and guidance for optimal rehabilitation. This study aimed to develop an international expert consensus for the management of HSI. A modified Delphi methodology and consensus process was used with an international expert panel, involving two rounds of online questionnaires and an intermediate round involving a consensus meeting. The initial information gathering round questionnaire was sent to 46 international experts, which comprised open-ended questions covering decision-making domains in HSI. Thematic analysis of responses outlined key domains, which were evaluated by a smaller international subgroup (n=15), comprising clinical academic sports medicine physicians, physiotherapists and orthopaedic surgeons in a consensus meeting. After group discussion around each domain, a series of consensus statements were prepared, debated and refined. A round 2 questionnaire was sent to 112 international hamstring experts to vote on these statements and determine level of agreement. Consensus threshold was set a priori at 70%. Expert response rates were 35/46 (76%) (first round), 15/35 (attendees/invitees to meeting day) and 99/112 (88.2%) for final survey round. Statements on rehabilitation reaching consensus centred around: exercise selection and dosage (78.8%-96.3% agreement), impact of the kinetic chain (95%), criteria to progress exercise (73%-92.7%), running and sprinting (83%-100%) in rehabilitation and criteria for return to sport (RTS) (78.3%-98.3%). Benchmarks for flexibility (40%) and strength (66.1%) and adjuncts to rehabilitation (68.9%) did not reach agreement. This consensus panel recommends individualised rehabilitation based on the athlete, sporting demands, involved muscle(s) and injury type and severity (89.8%). Early-stage rehab should avoid high strain loads and rates. Loading is important but with less consensus on optimum progression and dosage. This panel recommends rehabilitation progress based on capacity and symptoms, with pain thresholds dependent on activity, except pain-free criteria supported for sprinting (85.5%). Experts focus on the demands and capacity required for match play when deciding the rehabilitation end goal and timing of RTS (89.8%). The expert panellists in this study followed evidence on aspects of rehabilitation after HSI, suggesting rehabilitation prescription should be individualised, but clarified areas where evidence was lacking. Additional research is required to determine the optimal load dose, timing and criteria for HSI rehabilitation and the monitoring and testing metrics to determine safe rapid progression in rehabilitation and safe RTS. Further research would benefit optimising: prescription of running and sprinting, the application of adjuncts in rehabilitation and treatment of kinetic chain HSI factors.", "tags": ["Consensus de Londres", "Rééducation", "Sprint", "Critères RTS", "BJSM 2023"], "pdf_file": "pdfs/PMID_36650032.pdf", "pdf_source": "publisher_oa_pdf", "pdf_size_kb": 1231, "pubmed_url": "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/36650032/", "doi_url": "https://doi.org/10.1136/bjsports-2021-105384", "pmc_url": "", "article_url": "https://doi.org/10.1136/bjsports-2021-105384"}, {"id": "pmid-34471958", "pmid": "34471958", "pmcid": "", "doi": "10.1007/s00167-021-06718-1", "title": "Anatomical retraction of the semitendinosus muscle following harvest of the distal semitendinosus tendon for ACL reconstruction", "title_fr": "Rétraction anatomique du muscle semi-tendineux en IRM après prélèvement tendineux pour reconstruction du LCA", "authors_list": ["Speedtsberg MB", "Zebis MK", "Lauridsen HB", "Magnussen E", "Hölmich P"], "authors_short": "Speedtsberg MB, Zebis MK et al.", "journal": "Knee surgery, sports traumatology, arthroscopy : official journal of the ESSKA", "journal_short": "KSSTA", "impact_factor": 3.3, "year": 2021, "month_num": 9, "month_fr": "Sept.", "pub_date_fr": "Sept. 2021", "volume": "30", "issue": "5", "pages": "1706-1710", "category_id": "acl_graft_harvest", "category_label": "Ischio-Jambiers & Plastie du LCA (Prélèvement & Régénération)", "category_short": "Greffe DIDT/DT4 & Régénération", "category_color": "#e11d48", "modality": "IRM Bilatérale Comparative", "intervention_type": "Morbidité Site Donneur DIDT", "cochin_link": "Quantifie précisément en IRM bilatérale le raccourcissement définitif du corps charnu du semi-tendineux après prélèvement pour LCA : 81 mm en moyenne (soit 25 % de sa longueur), expliquant le déficit de force en flexion profonde (> 70–90°).", "summary_fr_short": "À 6,8 ans de recul moyen après prélèvement DIDT pour plastie du LCA, l'IRM bilatérale comparative montre une rétraction proximale moyenne de 81 mm (25 % de raccourcissement) du corps musculaire du semi-tendineux par rapport au côté sain (où la variation naturelle est < 10 mm).", "summary_fr_detailed": "En comparant par IRM panoramique des deux cuisses des sujets opérés d'un LCA par DIDT à un groupe témoin sain (chez qui l'asymétrie droite/gauche de longueur du semi-tendineux est toujours < 10 mm), les auteurs démontrent que toute rétraction > 10 mm est directement imputable au prélèvement chirurgical. Malgré la fréquente régénération d'un néo-tendon fibreux distal s'insérant plus haut sur le fascia poplité, le ventre musculaire du semi-tendineux reste raccourci de 8,1 cm (25 %), ce qui réduit son bras de levier en flexion profonde du genou chez les sportifs de combat ou de gymnastique.", "protocol_fr": "Prise en compte du raccourcissement du semi-tendineux (-25 %) dans le choix du greffon chez les athlètes nécessitant une force maximale en flexion profonde du genou (> 90°) ou en sprint.", "key_points_fr": ["Asymétrie physiologique naturelle du semi-tendineux entre les deux jambes toujours inférieure à 10 mm.", "Raccourcissement musculaire moyen de 81 mm (-25 %) du semi-tendineux persistant à près de 7 ans post-LCA.", "Explique biomécaniquement le déficit résiduel de flexion active profonde après prélèvement ST/Gracilis."], "abstract_en": "Purpose Retraction of semitendinosus muscle has been reported after reconstruction of the anterior cruciate ligament with semitendinosus/gracilis-graft. However, very little data exist on the natural variation in side-to-side length symmetry. The purpose of this study was to investigate the side-to-side asymmetry of semitendinosus muscle length in individuals with ACL reconstruction (ACLR) using the semitendinosus/gracilis-graft compared to a group of healthy control subjects to establish the level of retraction that can confidently be ascribed the surgery. Methods Eleven subjects aged 30 (19-39) years, with previous unilateral ACLR with the combined semitendinosus/gracilis tendon graft were recruited. Average follow-up was 6.8 years (0.3-13.0) after reconstruction. Ten healthy subjects aged 30 years (23-36) with no previous knee surgery served as controls. Bilateral magnetic resonance imaging (MRI) scans were obtained of the thigh from 60 mm below the knee joint and 700 mm proximal to this point with a slice thickness of 5 mm with 5 mm inter-slice distance. Semitendinosus length was measured on both legs between the distal and proximal musculotendinous junction of the semitendinosus. Length difference between legs was calculated for all participants. Percentage of shortening was expressed relative to the healthy leg. Results Subjects who had undergone ACLR had on average 81 mm (25%) shortening of the semitendinosus on the reconstructed leg compared to the non-reconstructed side. The healthy subjects all had less than 10 mm difference between legs ( Conclusion This study indicates that retraction larger than 10 mm is a consequence of the tendon harvest and not natural variation. It also supports that persistent retraction of the semitendinosus muscle occurs following harvest of the semitendinosus tendon for ACL graft. Level of evidence Level IV.", "tags": ["Prélèvement DIDT", "Semi-tendineux", "Rétraction musculaire", "IRM", "KSSTA 2022"], "pdf_file": null, "pdf_source": "external_link", "pdf_size_kb": null, "pubmed_url": "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/34471958/", "doi_url": "https://doi.org/10.1007/s00167-021-06718-1", "pmc_url": "", "article_url": "https://doi.org/10.1007/s00167-021-06718-1"}, {"id": "pmid-33855098", "pmid": "33855098", "pmcid": "PMC8013639", "doi": "10.1177/2325967121999643", "title": "Surgical Repair of Distal Biceps Femoris Avulsion Injuries in Professional Athletes", "title_fr": "Réparation chirurgicale des avulsions distales du biceps fémoral chez l'athlète professionnel", "authors_list": ["Thompson JW", "Plastow R", "Kayani B", "Moriarty P", "Asokan A", "Haddad FS"], "authors_short": "Thompson JW, Plastow R et al.", "journal": "Orthopaedic journal of sports medicine", "journal_short": "Orthop J Sports Med", "impact_factor": 2.6, "year": 2021, "month_num": 3, "month_fr": "Mars", "pub_date_fr": "Mars 2021", "volume": "9", "issue": "3", "pages": "2325967121999643", "category_id": "distal_tears_t_junction", "category_label": "Ruptures Distales & Jonction en T du Biceps Fémoral", "category_short": "Lésions Distales & Jonction T", "category_color": "#06b6d4", "modality": "Chirurgie Distale", "intervention_type": "Réinsertion Distale Biceps Fémoral", "cochin_link": "Série prospective de référence (équipe du Pr Fares Haddad, Londres) sur 22 athlètes d'élite : 100 % de retour au niveau professionnel antérieur en 16,7 semaines après réinsertion par ancres sur la tête fibulaire, avec 0 % de récidive.", "summary_fr_short": "Chez 22 sportifs professionnels (football, rugby) opérés en aigu (délai moyen 12 jours) d'une avulsion distale du biceps fémoral par ancres de suture, 100 % reprennent la compétition à leur niveau antérieur en 16,7 ± 8,7 semaines sans aucune récidive à 2 ans.", "summary_fr_detailed": "L'avulsion distale du tendon du biceps fémoral à son insertion sur la tête de la fibula est une lésion grave du point d'angle postéro-latéral du genou, exposant à une perte durable de force de flexion/rotation externe si elle est traitée orthopédiquement. Opérée précocement (en moyenne à J12) par réinsertion anatomique sur ancres avec repérage et protection systématique du nerf fibulaire commun (SPE), elle permet un retour intégral au sport professionnel en ~4 mois sans récidive ni complication neurologique.", "protocol_fr": "Abord postéro-latéral centré sur la tête fibulaire • Neurolyse et protection première du nerf fibulaire commun (SPE) au col de la fibula • Réinsertion anatomique de l'empreinte distale du biceps fémoral par ancres de suture.", "key_points_fr": ["100 % de retour au niveau sportif professionnel antérieur chez 22 athlètes d'élite.", "Délai moyen de retour à la compétition complète de 16,7 ± 8,7 semaines (~4 mois).", "Zéro récidive à 1 et 2 ans de suivi et restauration complète de la force isocinétique.", "Protection impérative du nerf fibulaire commun (SPE) lors de l'abord postéro-latéral."], "abstract_en": "Background Understanding the optimal management of distal biceps femoris avulsion injuries is critical for restoring preinjury function, restoring hamstring muscle strength, increasing range of motion, and minimizing risk of complications and recurrence. Due to the rarity of these injuries, prognosis and outcomes within the literature are limited to case reports and small case series. Purpose To assess the effect of surgical repair for acute distal avulsion injuries of the biceps femoris tendon on (1) return to preinjury level of sporting function and (2) time to return to preinjury level of sporting function, patient satisfaction, and complications. Study design Case series; Level of evidence, 4. Methods This prospective single-surgeon study included 22 elite athletes (18 men [82%], 4 women [18%]; mean age, 26 years; age range, 17-35 years; mean body mass index, 25.3 ± 4.1 kg/m 2 ) undergoing primary suture anchor repair of avulsion injuries of the distal biceps femoris confirmed on preoperative magnetic resonance imaging. Predefined outcomes relating to time for return to sporting activity, patient satisfaction, complications, and injury recurrence were recorded at regular intervals after surgery. Minimum follow-up time was 12 months (range, 12.0-26.0 months) from the date of surgery. Results The mean time from injury to surgical intervention was 12 days (range, 2-28 days). All study patients returned to their preinjury level of sporting activity, predominately professional soccer or rugby. Mean time from surgical intervention to return to full sporting activity was 16.7 ± 8.7 weeks. At 1- and 2-year follow-up, all study patients were still participating at their preinjury level of sporting activity. There was no incidence of primary injury recurrence, and no patients required further operation to the biceps origin. Conclusion Surgical repair of acute avulsion injuries of the distal biceps femoris facilitated early return to preinjury level of function with low risk of recurrence, low complication rate, and high patient satisfaction in elite athletes. Suture anchor repair of these injuries should be considered a reliable treatment option in athletes with high functional demands to permit an early return to sport with restoration of hamstring strength.", "tags": ["Biceps fémoral distal", "Tête de la fibula", "Athlète professionnel", "Nerf fibulaire commun", "OJSM 2021"], "pdf_file": null, "pdf_source": "external_link", "pdf_size_kb": null, "pubmed_url": "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/33855098/", "doi_url": "https://doi.org/10.1177/2325967121999643", "pmc_url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8013639/", "article_url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8013639/"}, {"id": "pmid-32736504", "pmid": "32736504", "pmcid": "", "doi": "10.1177/0363546520938679", "title": "Surgical Repair of Distal Musculotendinous T Junction Injuries of the Biceps Femoris", "title_fr": "Réparation chirurgicale des lésions aiguës de la jonction musculo-tendineuse distale en T (T-junction) du biceps fémoral", "authors_list": ["Kayani B", "Ayuob A", "Begum F", "Singh S", "Haddad FS"], "authors_short": "Kayani B, Ayuob A et al.", "journal": "The American journal of sports medicine", "journal_short": "Am J Sports Med", "impact_factor": 4.2, "year": 2020, "month_num": 8, "month_fr": "Août", "pub_date_fr": "Août 2020", "volume": "48", "issue": "10", "pages": "2456-2464", "category_id": "distal_tears_t_junction", "category_label": "Ruptures Distales & Jonction en T du Biceps Fémoral", "category_short": "Lésions Distales & Jonction T", "category_color": "#06b6d4", "modality": "Chirurgie Distale", "intervention_type": "Suture de la Jonction en T (DMTJ)", "cochin_link": "Alors que le traitement conservateur des lésions de la jonction en T du biceps fémoral s'accompagne de 54 % de récidives, la réparation chirurgicale directe chez 34 professionnels permet 100 % de retour au jeu en 11,7 semaines avec 0 % de rechute.", "summary_fr_short": "Étude prospective de 34 athlètes professionnels opérés d'une rupture aiguë de la jonction musculo-tendineuse distale en T (DMTJ) du biceps fémoral : 100 % de retour au niveau pré-lésionnel en 11,7 ± 3,6 semaines et aucune récidive à 28,4 mois de recul moyen.", "summary_fr_detailed": "La jonction musculo-tendineuse distale en T (DMTJ) du biceps fémoral, zone de confluence entre le chef long (innervé par le contingent tibial) et le chef court (innervé par le contingent fibulaire commun), est tristement célèbre pour son taux de récidive supérieur à 50 % sous traitement médical. Dans cette série prospective (34 professionnels), la remise en tension et la suture anatomique de l'aponévrose en T réduisent le délai de retour au jeu à moins de 3 mois (11,7 semaines) et suppriment le risque de rechute (0 % à plus de 2 ans).", "protocol_fr": "Voie postéro-latérale distale de cuisse • Évacuation de l'hématome et débridement du tissu cicatriciel • Suture anatomique bord à bord en paletot des feuillets aponévrotiques des chefs long et court du biceps fémoral.", "key_points_fr": ["34 athlètes professionnels suivis pendant 28,4 mois en moyenne.", "Retour complet à la compétition en 11,7 ± 3,6 semaines (< 3 mois).", "Taux de récidive ramené à 0 % (contre > 50 % dans la littérature non chirurgicale).", "100 % des joueurs satisfaits ou très satisfaits avec récupération symétrique de la force à 3 mois."], "abstract_en": "Background Nonoperative management of injuries to the distal musculotendinous T junction of the biceps femoris is associated with variable periods of rehabilitation and high risk of recurrence. To our knowledge, the efficacy of operative treatment in patients with these acute injuries has not been previously reported. Hypothesis Surgical repair of injuries to the distal musculotendinous T junction of the biceps femoris would enable return to preinjury level of sport with low risk of recurrence. Study design Case series; Level of evidence, 4. Methods This prospective single-surgeon study included 34 professional athletes (mean age, 26.4 ± 3.1 years; 31 male [91.2%]; 3 female [8.8%]; body mass index, 25 ± 2.0 kg/m 2 ) undergoing primary surgical repair of acute injuries to the distal musculotendinous T junction of the biceps femoris. All study patients underwent a standardized postoperative rehabilitation program. Predefined study outcomes relating to time for return to sporting activity, patient satisfaction, range of motion, hamstring muscle strength, passive range of motion, functional progress, and complications were recorded at regular intervals after surgery. Mean follow-up time was 28.4 months (range, 24.0-36.3 months) from date of surgery. Results All study patients returned to their preinjury level of sporting activity. Mean time from surgical repair to full sporting activity was 11.7 ± 3.6 weeks. No patients had recurrence of the primary injury. At 1-year follow-up, 18 patients (52.9%) were very satisfied and 16 patients (47.1%) were satisfied with the outcomes of their surgery. At 3 months after surgery, patients had improved mean passive straight leg raise (69.7° ± 11.7° vs 24.1° ± 7.4°; P P P P P P Conclusion Surgical repair of acute injuries to the distal musculotendinous T junction of the biceps femoris is associated with high patient satisfaction, increased muscle strength, improved functional outcome scores, and high return to preinjury level of sporting activity with low risk of recurrence at short-term follow-up.", "tags": ["Jonction en T (T-junction)", "Biceps fémoral distal", "Chirurgie", "Zéro récidive", "AJSM 2020"], "pdf_file": null, "pdf_source": "external_link", "pdf_size_kb": null, "pubmed_url": "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/32736504/", "doi_url": "https://doi.org/10.1177/0363546520938679", "pmc_url": "", "article_url": "https://doi.org/10.1177/0363546520938679"}, {"id": "pmid-28795071", "pmid": "28795071", "pmcid": "PMC5524253", "doi": "10.1177/2325967117714998", "title": "Distal Musculotendinous T Junction Injuries of the Biceps Femoris: An MRI Case Review", "title_fr": "Lésions de la jonction musculo-tendineuse distale en T du biceps fémoral : sémiologie IRM et haut risque de récidive (54 %)", "authors_list": ["Entwisle T", "Ling Y", "Splatt A", "Brukner P", "Connell D"], "authors_short": "Entwisle T, Ling Y et al.", "journal": "Orthopaedic journal of sports medicine", "journal_short": "Orthop J Sports Med", "impact_factor": 2.6, "year": 2017, "month_num": 7, "month_fr": "Juil.", "pub_date_fr": "Juil. 2017", "volume": "5", "issue": "7", "pages": "2325967117714998", "category_id": "distal_tears_t_junction", "category_label": "Ruptures Distales & Jonction en T du Biceps Fémoral", "category_short": "Lésions Distales & Jonction T", "category_color": "#06b6d4", "modality": "IRM Diagnostique", "intervention_type": "Sémiologie IRM Jonction en T", "cochin_link": "Article fondateur d'Entwisle, Brukner et Connell décrivant l'anatomie IRM en T de la confluence distale des chefs long et court du biceps fémoral et démontrant son taux redoutable de récidive (54 %).", "summary_fr_short": "Analyse de 106 IRM chez 55 patients présentant une lésion de la jonction distale en T (DMTJ) du biceps fémoral : le chef long est touché isolément dans 51 % des cas et les deux chefs dans 43 %. Le taux de récidive sous traitement conservateur atteint 54 %, dont 76 % dans les 3 premiers mois.", "summary_fr_detailed": "En coupe axiale IRM au tiers distal de la cuisse, l'aponévrose distale du chef long et celle du chef court du biceps fémoral fusionnent selon une configuration en « T ». En raison de la double innervation (nerf tibial pour le chef long, nerf fibulaire commun pour le chef court) et des contraintes de cisaillement lors du sprint, les lésions de cette jonction en T récidivent dans 54 % des cas, et dans 86 % des cas la rechute est de grade égal ou plus sévère que la lésion initiale.", "protocol_fr": "Analyse fine sur les coupes axiales T2/DP-FS distales des deux bras du 'T' aponévrotique (composante du chef long, composante du chef court, ou dissociation complète des deux).", "key_points_fr": ["Entité anatomique et clinique distincte : 51 % d'atteinte du chef long seul, 43 % d'atteinte combinée des deux chefs.", "Taux de récidive exceptionnellement élevé de 54 % sous traitement rééducatif seul.", "76 % des rechutes surviennent dans les 3 mois suivant la reprise et 86 % sont au moins aussi graves que la lésion initiale."], "abstract_en": "Background Injury to the distal musculotendinous T junction (DMTJ) of the biceps femoris is a distinct clinical entity that behaves differently from other hamstring injuries due to its complex, multicomponent anatomy and dual innervation. Injury in this region demonstrates a particularly high rate of recurrence, even with prolonged rehabilitation times. Purpose To describe the anatomy of the DMTJ of the biceps femoris and analyze the injury patterns seen on magnetic resonance imaging (MRI) to aid prognosis and rehabilitation and minimize the risk of recurrence. Study design Cross-sectional study; Level of evidence, 3. Methods Acute injury to the DMTJ of the biceps femoris was identified in 106 MRI examinations from 55 patients at a single institution. Each injury was classified as involving the long head, the short head, or both components of the DMTJ, with each component individually graded. Injuries were classified as recurrent if there was a previous MRI demonstrating an acute injury to the DMTJ or if there was scarring present at the site of an acute injury. Results Of the 106 acute injuries to the DMTJ of the biceps femoris, isolated injury to the long head component was the most common (51%), with both components involved in [round 42.5% to 43%] of cases. Isolated injury to the short head component accounted for 7% of cases. The recurrence rate for reinjury to the DMTJ was 54% in this series. The date of prior injury was known in 45 of 57 recurrent cases, with 34 of these reoccurring within 3 months (76%) and 40 reoccurring within 12 months (89%). The recurrent injury was of a higher grade than the prior injury in 22 of 44 instances (50%), the same grade in 16 instances (36%), and a lower grade in 6 instances (14%). Thus, 86% of recurrent injuries were of the same or higher grade than prior injury. Conclusion These results suggest that high-risk muscle injuries, such as that to the DMTJ of the biceps femoris, should be evaluated using MRI to determine the structural components involved and to assess the extent and severity of injury.", "tags": ["Jonction en T (DMTJ)", "IRM", "Récidive", "Double innervation", "OJSM 2017"], "pdf_file": "pdfs/PMID_28795071.pdf", "pdf_source": "publisher_oa_pdf", "pdf_size_kb": 517, "pubmed_url": "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/28795071/", "doi_url": "https://doi.org/10.1177/2325967117714998", "pmc_url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5524253/", "article_url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5524253/"}];
